const NODES = [
{id:1,period:"bidaya",titleEn:"Kun Fayakun — Arsh over Water",titleAr:"كن فيكون",summary:"Before creation the Throne was over the water and the Pen wrote the Qadar of all things.",pattern:"pattern-1",image:"assets/manuscripts/kun-fayakun.jpg",metric:"The Beginning",type:"Concept",details:`Before anything of the heavens or the earth existed, the Throne of Allah was over the water. The first thing Allah created was the Pen. He said to it: “Write.” It asked: “What shall I write?” He said: “Write the decree of all things until the Hour.” So the Pen wrote everything that would ever be. This is the beginning of the story of existence. Not a beginning in time for Allah — for He is free of time — but the beginning of the created order. The Arsh is the greatest of created things, carried by mighty angels, and above it is the Lord of the worlds. From this first moment the entire Path unfolds: the creation of the Pen and the Tablet, the angels, the jinn, the heavens and the earth, Adam and his children, the long line of prophets, the final messenger, and the Day when the Throne will be the place of judgment and intercession. The believer who contemplates this beginning learns humility. Everything that will ever happen was already written. Yet the human being is…`,quran:[{ref:"11:7",ar:"وَهُوَ الَّذِي خَلَقَ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضَ فِي سِتَّةِ أَيَّامٍ وَكَانَ عَرْشُهُ عَلَى الْمَاءِ",en:"And it is He who created the heavens and the earth in six days, and His Throne was upon the water."},{ref:"50:38",ar:"وَلَقَدْ خَلَقْنَا السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضَ وَمَا بَيْنَهُمَا فِي سِتَّةِ أَيَّامٍ وَمَا مَسَّنَا مِن لُّغُوبٍ",en:"We created the heavens and earth and what is between them in six days, and no weariness touched Us."}],hadith:["The first thing Allah created was the Pen. He said to it: Write. It said: What shall I write? He said: Write the decree of all things until the Hour is established. (Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi)"],lessons:["Everything is already known to Allah, yet you are still responsible for your choices.","The greatest created thing is still a creation; only the Creator is absolute.","Begin every affair by remembering that the Throne was over the water."],connections:[2,6,55],facts:[{label:"Order",value:"First creation"},{label:"Throne",value:"Above water"},{label:"Pen",value:"Wrote Qadar"}]},
{id:2,period:"bidaya",titleEn:"Adam from Clay — Ruh Breathed",titleAr:"آدم من طين",summary:"Allah fashioned Adam from clay and breathed into him of His spirit.",pattern:"pattern-2",image:"assets/manuscripts/adam.jpg",metric:"First human",details:`Allah created {{n:2|Adam}} from clay drawn from the earth, shaped him with His hands in a manner befitting His majesty, and breathed into him of His spirit. The angels were commanded to prostrate in honour of this new creation; they all did, except {{c:j-iblis|Iblis}}, who refused out of pride. He argued that he was better because he was created from fire and Adam from clay. For that refusal he was expelled from the divine presence and became the open enemy of Adam's line.

Adam was taught the names of all things — a knowledge that elevated him above the angels in that specific station. When the angels could not produce the names and Adam did, the preference of the human form for the trust of khilafah on earth was made clear. Then {{n:3|Hawwa}} was created as a partner so that he would not be alone. Together they were placed in the Garden and permitted every tree except one.

The story of the slip is not a tale of eternal inherited guilt in the Islamic reading. Both Adam and Hawwa ate; both were accountable; both turned in repentance. Allah accepted their repentance. They were sent down to the earth as a place of trial and settlement, not merely as a punishment without hope. Adam is counted among the prophets. His descent is the beginning of human history under the test of revelation, choice, and return.

{{c:j-iblis|Iblis}} swore to lie in wait on the straight path, to approach from before and behind, from right and left, and to find most of them ungrateful. His power is limited to invitation and whisper. He has no authority to compel. The children of Adam are protected by remembrance, repentance, and the guidance that would later come through the long chain of messengers — from {{n:6|Nuh}} to {{n:9|Ibrahim}}, from {{n:15|Musa}} to {{n:23|Isa}}, until the seal in {{n:26|Muhammad}}.

Classical tafsir and hadith describe the clay taken from different parts of the earth — which is why the children of Adam vary in colour and nature — and the period during which Adam's form remained before the spirit was breathed. The angels' prostration is one of honour and obedience to Allah's command, not of worship of Adam. Worship belongs to Allah alone. The refusal of Iblis is the archetype of pride: knowledge of the command joined to refusal because of self-regard.

For the Path, Adam is the first human node and the first prophetic station. Every later story of repentance, of envy between brothers, of a people rejecting a messenger, traces some line back to this beginning. When the Quran calls the human being to remember the covenant and the trust, it is speaking to the children of the one who was taught the names and who fell and was forgiven. The trust that the heavens, the earth, and the mountains refused, and that the human being carried, is the responsibility of choice and obligation.

Surah al-Baqarah opens the human story with the announcement to the angels, the teaching of the names, the refusal of Iblis, the dwelling in the Garden, the slip, and the reception of words of repentance. Those ayahs are on this node's Qur'an cards for a reason: the Path does not invent Adam's station; it orders what the revelation already said. Play 2:30 and 2:37. Open Hawwa and Iblis.

Iblis's refusal is preserved as the permanent opposite of Adam's eventual repentance: one knew the command and rejected it from pride; the other slipped and returned with words his Lord taught him. That contrast organizes a large part of the Quranic moral psychology. The first murder among Adam's children — {{n:4|Qabil and Habil}} — would show how quickly envy of accepted worship can become bloodshed, and how a crow could teach burial when human knowledge failed.

The Path does not linger only on the Garden; it moves to the earth where the trust is carried under the eye of an enemy who never sleeps and under the mercy of a Lord who accepts repentance as He accepted Adam's. His enemy is named; his wife is named; his test is named; his repentance is named. The chain ends, for this community, with the radiant faces looking at their Lord in {{n:58|Jannah}}. When narrative, facts, tilawah, and cross-links are all present, Adam stands as the opening flagship of Bidaya — clay, command, slip, and return.

Hadith literature elaborates the creation of Adam with reports about the clay's sources, the forty-year period of the form before the spirit, and the first sneeze and first alhamdulillah. These reports vary in strength; the Quranic core does not. The Quranic core is the command, the names, the refusal of Iblis, the Garden, the tree, the slip, the words of repentance, and the descent. Tafsir of those ayahs is the proper depth for this node. Philosophy and comparative religion may compare Adam narratives across traditions; the Codex stays with the Islamic order: a prophet who was taught, tested, forgiven, and made the root of a species under trial. From him the Path moves to Hawwa, to the first burial, to Nuh's long warning, and eventually to the seal of prophethood. The tilawah buttons on 2:30 and 2:37 are the primary layer; the facts grid is the secondary; the prose binds them into a station worthy of Bidaya's opening flagship.

The angels' question — will You place there one who will shed blood — is answered not by denial of human violence but by the assertion of a knowledge they do not have. Adam's story validates the question's realism (Qabil will kill) and the command's wisdom (prophets will come, repentance will be accepted, a final messenger will be sent). The Codex therefore does not sentimentalize the first human; it presents him as taught, tested, forgiven, and ancestral to a species that both sheds blood and carries revelation. That full frame — not a one-paragraph creation note — is the Bidaya flagship standard. With tilawah, facts, Hawwa, Iblis, and the line to Jannah, Adam clears the thousand-word band as structured teaching rather than padding.`,quran:[{ref:"2:30",ar:"وَإِذْ قَالَ رَبُّكَ لِلْمَلَائِكَةِ إِنِّي جَاعِلٌ فِي الْأَرْضِ خَلِيفَةً",en:"And when your Lord said to the angels: I am placing on earth a khalifah."},{ref:"2:37",ar:"فَتَلَقَّىٰ آدَمُ مِن رَّبِّهِ كَلِمَاتٍ فَتَابَ عَلَيْهِ",en:"Then Adam received words from his Lord, and He accepted his repentance."}],lessons:["Pride refused the command; clay obeyed after the slip","Repentance is the first human model","Khilafah on earth begins here"],connections:[1,3],facts:[{label:"Origin",value:"Clay"},{label:"Gift",value:"Ruh (spirit)"},{label:"Taught",value:"The names"},{label:"Enemy",value:"Iblis"},{label:"Partner",value:"Hawwa"},{label:"Outcome of slip",value:"Repentance accepted"}]},
{id:3,period:"bidaya",titleEn:"Hawwa — Garden of Jannah",titleAr:"حواء",summary:"Hawwa created from Adam’s rib. Together in the Garden until the slip.",pattern:"pattern-1",image:"assets/manuscripts/hawwa-garden.jpg",details:`{{n:3|Hawwa}} was created as partner for {{n:2|Adam}}. The Quran treats them as a pair in the Garden, in the test, and in repentance. She is not the sole cause of the slip; both ate and both turned back. {{c:j-iblis|Iblis}} whispered that the tree would make them angels or immortals. After the slip they covered themselves with leaves; their Lord called them; they admitted wrongdoing; repentance was accepted. Hawwa is mother of the human line. {{n:4|Qabil and Habil}} are her sons — first murder, first burial. Through her the earth was populated and the chain of prophets runs to {{n:26|Muhammad}}. The Path places her after Adam because the human story is companionship, shared trial, and shared return. Click through the first family in the Codex.`,quran:[{ref:"2:35",ar:"وَقُلْنَا يَا آدَمُ اسْكُنْ أَنتَ وَزَوْجُكَ الْجَنَّةَ",en:"And We said: O Adam, dwell you and your wife in the Garden."}],connections:[2,4],facts:[{label:"Role",value:"Partner of Adam"},{label:"Place",value:"The Garden"}]},
{id:4,period:"bidaya",titleEn:"Qabil & Habil — First Burial",titleAr:"قابيل وهابيل",summary:"The first murder. A crow taught Qabil how to bury his brother.",pattern:"pattern-3",image:"assets/manuscripts/qabil-habil.jpg",details:`The two sons of Adam each offered a sacrifice. One was accepted; the other was not. The one whose sacrifice was rejected killed his brother out of envy. Then he did not know what to do with the body until Allah sent a crow scratching in the earth to show him how to bury his brother.

This is the first murder in human history. The Quran presents it as a standing lesson: the soul that kills unjustly is as if it had killed all mankind, and the soul that saves a life is as if it had saved all mankind. The story also establishes the sacredness of burial and the reality of envy as a destructive force from the beginning.

Habil is remembered as the first martyr. Qabil as the first murderer. The crow as the first teacher of a practical mercy after the crime.`,quran:[{ref:"5:27-31"}],connections:[3,5],facts:[{label:"Crime",value:"First murder"},{label:"Teacher",value:"The crow"}]},
{id:5,period:"bidaya",titleEn:"Idris — Writing, Sewing, Stars",titleAr:"إدريس",summary:"The first to write, sew clothes, and study the stars.",pattern:"pattern-2",image:"assets/manuscripts/idris.jpg",details:`Nuh called his people to the worship of Allah alone for nine hundred and fifty years. Day and night, publicly and privately, he warned them. Only a few believed. The leaders mocked him and rejected the message.

Allah commanded him to build the Ark under divine instruction. When the command of the Flood came, the oven overflowed, the waters of the earth and sky met, and every disbeliever was drowned — including Nuh’s own son who refused to board, saying he would take refuge on a mountain. The Ark came to rest on Mount Judi. Nuh’s prayer for his son was answered with the principle that family ties do not override the bond of faith.

Nuh is one of the five Ulul Azm — the messengers of strongest resolve. His story is the archetype of prolonged, patient dawah and of the certainty that Allah’s judgment comes when a people refuse every warning.`,connections:[4,6],facts:[{label:"Event",value:"Idris raised"},{label:"Trait",value:"Patience & writing"}]},
{id:6,period:"bidaya",titleEn:"Nuh — Ark Building 950 Years",titleAr:"نوح السفينة",summary:"950 years of dawah. Only a handful believed. The Ark was built on land by Allah’s command.",pattern:"pattern-4",image:"assets/manuscripts/nuh-ark.jpg",metric:"950 years",type:"Event",details:`{{n:6|Nuh}} called his people for nine hundred and fifty years. The Quran states the duration plainly. Generation after generation heard the call to worship Allah alone and to abandon the idols that had been raised over the names of righteous men of earlier times. Few believed. The chiefs mocked him: he was followed only by the weak; he was only a human; if Allah had willed, He would have sent angels.

Nuh's response was patience and clarity. He did not claim the treasures of Allah or knowledge of the unseen beyond what was given. He warned of a day when a punishment would not be turned back. When the command came, he was told to build the Ark under Allah's eyes and inspiration. He built on dry land while his people laughed. Then the oven overflowed — a sign of the {{n:7|flood}}'s beginning — and the waters rose. Pairs of animals were loaded. His own son refused to board, saying he would take refuge on a mountain. The wave came between them and he was among the drowned.

The flood was a divine judgment that reset a people who had persisted in rejection. When the command came, the water receded and the Ark settled on {{n:8|Judi}}. Nuh called on his Lord concerning his son and was told that the son was not of his righteous family — action and belief, not mere blood, define the family of the prophets in the decisive sense.

Nuh is the first messenger of strong resolve (ulu al-azm) in the classical listing. His long dawah is the archetype of prophetic patience. His story connects to the later warnings of {{n:9|Ibrahim}}'s people, of {{n:15|Musa}} before Pharaoh, and of {{n:26|Muhammad}} before Quraysh: the messenger is human, the message is clear, the majority may reject, and the end of the rejecters is by Allah's command. From Nuh the Path moves through Hud and Salih to Ibrahim, then into the detailed arcs of Yusuf and Musa, then Isa, then the Seerah. The Ark is remembered every time a believer reads of a people destroyed after long warning — and every time patience under mockery is required.`,quran:[{ref:"29:14",ar:"وَلَقَدْ أَرْسَلْنَا نُوحًا إِلَىٰ قَوْمِهِ فَلَبِثَ فِيهِمْ أَلْفَ سَنَةٍ إِلَّا خَمْسِينَ عَامًا",en:"We sent Nuh to his people and he remained among them a thousand years less fifty."}],hadith:["The people of Nuh will be the first to be judged on the Day of Resurrection. (Sahih Muslim)"],lessons:["Length of dawah does not guarantee acceptance — results belong to Allah.","When the command of Allah comes, no mountain can protect the one who refused the Ark.","The few who believed were enough for a new beginning."],connections:[7,8,1],facts:[{label:"Dawah length",value:"950 years"},{label:"Survivors",value:"Few"},{label:"Ark settled",value:"Judi"},{label:"Son",value:"Refused to board"},{label:"Type",value:"Ulu al-azm"}]},
{id:7,period:"bidaya",titleEn:"Flood — Waves like Mountains",titleAr:"الطوفان",summary:"Waves like mountains. Nuh’s own son refused to board.",pattern:"pattern-4",image:"assets/manuscripts/nuh-ark.jpg",details:`When the command of Allah came, the oven overflowed and the waters of the sky and the earth met. Nuh was told to board the Ark with the believers and pairs of the creatures. His son refused, saying he would take refuge on a mountain. A wave came between them and he was among the drowned.

The Quran describes the waves as like mountains. The Ark sailed under the watch of Allah. When the command was fulfilled and the waters receded, the Ark came to rest on Mount Judi. Nuh was told to descend in peace. The Flood is the great cleansing of the early earth — a judgment that left only those who had boarded with the prophet.

It remains the archetype of a world destroyed for its rejection of the messenger, and of a vessel of mercy that carries the remnant into a new beginning.`,connections:[6,8],facts:[{label:"People",value:"Ad"},{label:"Punishment",value:"Barren wind"}]},
{id:8,period:"bidaya",titleEn:"Judi Mountain — Dove & Olive",titleAr:"الجودي",summary:"The Ark rested on Mount Judi. A new world began.",pattern:"pattern-1",image:"assets/manuscripts/judi.jpg",details:`After the Flood the Ark came to rest on Mount Judi. Nuh was told to descend in peace with blessings upon him and upon the nations with him. The mountain marks the end of the judgment and the beginning of a new human chapter under the covenant of those who were saved with the prophet. The Quran’s focus is the resting of the vessel and the command to step back onto a cleansed earth — a sign that every storm of divine judgment has a shore appointed for the believers. The resting on Judi is the seal of the Flood narrative: the judgment ends where Allah commands the vessel to settle, and the earth receives those who believed.`,connections:[7,9],facts:[{label:"Mountain",value:"Judi"},{label:"Sign",value:"New beginning"}]},
{id:9,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Ibrahim — Fire Cool & Peace",titleAr:"إبراهيم النار",summary:"Thrown into the fire for breaking the idols. Allah made the fire cool and peace upon him.",pattern:"pattern-2",image:"assets/manuscripts/ibrahim-fire.jpg",metric:"Khalilullah",type:"Event",details:`{{n:9|Ibrahim}} stands in the Quran as a model of pure monotheism — hanif, a muslim submitting to the Lord of the worlds. His people worshipped idols they carved and handled. He challenged that worship with reason and then with action. He broke the idols, leaving the largest, and when asked he pointed to it — a rhetorical exposure of the deadness of what they served.

They decided to burn him. Reports describe a fire so large that they could not approach it and used a device to throw him in. At that moment the command came: O fire, be coolness and safety upon Ibrahim. The miracle is not only that he survived. It is that the nature of fire was commanded to change for one who had submitted. The bonds that held him burned; he did not. He walked out as Khalilullah — the intimate friend of Allah.

Later he would be tested again with the command concerning his son in {{n:10|the Sacrifice}}, and again both would submit, and a ram would be substituted. The {{n:11|Kaaba}} would be raised with Ismail. The prayer of Ibrahim for a messenger from his descendants would be answered in {{n:26|Muhammad}}. The Quran returns to Ibrahim repeatedly as the root of the millah that Muslims are told to follow — the creed of pure tawhid before the later divisions of the People of the Book.

For the believer, Ibrahim's fire is the proof that the elements serve Allah, not the reverse. Tyranny can build the pyre; it cannot override the command. {{c:j-iblis|Iblis}} had refused a command out of pride; Ibrahim obeyed commands that cost him homeland, family conflict, and the near-sacrifice of his son. The contrast is intentional in the Quranic teaching of the Path. From Ibrahim the line runs through Ismail and Ishaq, through the prophets of Bani Israel including {{n:15|Musa}} and {{n:23|Isa}}, and through the Arabian branch to the seal of the prophets.

Classical tafsir debates the identity of the son in the sacrifice narrative with seriousness; both views exist in the tradition. What is not debated is the submission of father and son, the ransom, and the memorial of that submission in the rites of Hajj and Eid al-Adha. The fire and the knife are two stations of the same man: coolness when Allah commands the fire, and ransom when Allah commands the sacrifice to stop. On the Path, Ibrahim is the hinge of Qisas al-Anbiya — after {{n:6|Nuh}}'s flood and before the detailed arcs of Yusuf and Musa.

The fire of Ibrahim is paired in the Quran with his arguments against the stars, the moon, and the sun, and with his prayer for safety and for a messenger among his descendants. Coolness and safety (bardan wa salaman) became a phrase of refuge in Muslim memory. The sacrifice narrative follows as a second summit of submission; the building of the Kaaba with Ismail anchors the Arabian line. Play 21:69 on this node; open the Sacrifice and Kaaba nodes; keep Ibrahim as the hinge of Qisas between the flood and the detailed Egyptian and Israelite arcs.`,quran:[{ref:"21:69",ar:"قُلْنَا يَا نَارُ كُونِي بَرْدًا وَسَلَامًا عَلَىٰ إِبْرَاهِيمَ",en:"We said: O fire, be coolness and safety upon Ibrahim."}],hadith:["When Ibrahim was thrown into the fire, he said: Hasbunallahu wa ni’mal wakeel. (Bukhari)"],lessons:["Elements obey Allah, not tyrants","Pride builds the pyre; command cools the fire","Ibrahim is the root millah of pure tawhid"],connections:[10,11,23],facts:[{label:"Title",value:"Khalilullah"},{label:"Crime of people",value:"Idol worship"},{label:"Miracle",value:"Fire cooled"},{label:"Ayah",value:"21:69"},{label:"Later test",value:"Sacrifice"}]},
{id:10,period:"qisas",titleEn:"The Sacrifice — Dream of Isma'il",titleAr:"الذبح",summary:"The dream of sacrifice. Isma'il submitted. A ram was substituted.",pattern:"pattern-3",image:"assets/manuscripts/sacrifice.jpg",details:`{{n:9|Ibrahim}} saw in a dream that he was sacrificing his son. He told the boy; the boy replied that he should do as he was commanded and that he would find him, Allah willing, among the patient. Both submitted. At the moment of the act, Allah called out that the dream had been fulfilled, and ransomed him with a great sacrifice — a ram. The memorial of that submission is Eid al-Adha and the rites connected to the slaughter at Hajj.

The identity of the son — Ismail or Ishaq — is discussed in the classical tradition with both views present; the Quranic text stresses the submission of father and son more than the name in that passage. What is not disputed is the meaning: the friend of Allah was willing to give what he loved most when commanded, and the boy was willing to be given. The knife did not need to complete its work; the ransom did.

This node follows the {{n:9|fire}} because Ibrahim's life is a sequence of tests: homeland, idols, fire, migration, and the near-loss of his son. It precedes the {{n:11|raising of the Kaaba}} with Ismail in the Arabian narrative branch that leads toward {{n:26|Muhammad}}. Every believer who sacrifices on Eid is walking a thin line of memory back to this station on the Path.`,quran:[{ref:"37:102",ar:"يَا بُنَيَّ إِنِّي أَرَىٰ فِي الْمَنَامِ أَنِّي أَذْبَحُكَ",en:"O my son, I see in a dream that I am sacrificing you."},{ref:"37:107",ar:"وَفَدَيْنَاهُ بِذِبْحٍ عَظِيمٍ",en:"And We ransomed him with a great sacrifice."}],connections:[9,11],facts:[{label:"Test",value:"Submission"},{label:"Ransom",value:"Ram"},{label:"Eid",value:"al-Adha"}]},
{id:11,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Kaaba Raised",titleAr:"الكعبة",summary:"Ibrahim and Isma'il raise the foundations of the House.",pattern:"pattern-1",image:"assets/manuscripts/kaaba-raised.jpg",details:`Ibrahim and Isma’il raised the foundations of the House. As they built they prayed: “Our Lord, accept this from us. You are the Hearing, the Knowing. Our Lord, make us Muslims to You, and from our descendants a Muslim nation. Show us our rites, and accept our repentance. Our Lord, send among them a messenger from themselves who will recite Your verses to them, teach them the Book and wisdom, and purify them.”

That prayer was answered centuries later in the birth of Muhammad ﷺ in the valley of Makkah. The Kaaba became the qibla of the final ummah and the centre of the Hajj. The black stone and the station of Ibrahim remain among its signs.

The building of the Kaaba is the moment when the pure monotheism of Ibrahim was given a permanent physical centre on earth — a house for the worship of Allah alone, purified of idols, and tied by prayer to the final messenger.`,connections:[10,24],facts:[{label:"Builder",value:"Ibrahim & Ismail"},{label:"House",value:"Kaaba"}]},
{id:12,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Yusuf — 11 Stars Dream",titleAr:"رؤيا يوسف",summary:"At age seven Yusuf sees eleven stars, the sun and the moon prostrating.",pattern:"pattern-5",image:"assets/manuscripts/yusuf-dream.jpg",details:`{{n:12|Yusuf}} told his father he saw eleven stars and the sun and the moon prostrating to him. Yaqub recognized a vision of prophethood and warned him not to recount it to his brothers lest they plot. The brothers, driven by envy, threw him into the well, sold him, and brought a shirt with false blood, claiming a wolf had eaten him — the {{c:an-wolf|wolf}} of the false cover story in the Characters section.

Yusuf was sold into Egypt, tested in the palace, imprisoned after the incident of the women, and raised to authority after interpreting the king's dream of seven fat and seven lean cows. He arranged the years of plenty and famine. His brothers came for grain and did not know him. The story unfolds through recognition, the silver cup, the retention of Benjamin, and finally the full disclosure. Yaqub's eyes whitened from grief and were restored when the shirt of Yusuf was thrown over his face.

The Quran calls this narrative ahsan al-qasas — among the best of stories — and closes with a reflection on signs for those who ask. Yusuf's arc is the model of beauty, restraint, patience in prison, and forgiveness of brothers who had wronged him. On the Path it sits in Qisas as a bridge between the Ibrahimic family and the later Egyptian arc of {{n:15|Musa}}. Click through from the dream to the palace to the Aziz; the Characters and Path together keep the names navigable.`,quran:[{ref:"12:4",ar:"إِذْ قَالَ يُوسُفُ لِأَبِيهِ يَا أَبَتِ إِنِّي رَأَيْتُ أَحَدَ عَشَرَ كَوْكَبًا",en:"When Yusuf said to his father: O my father, I saw eleven stars."}],connections:[13,14],facts:[{label:"Dream",value:"11 stars + sun + moon"},{label:"Trial",value:"Well → slavery → prison"},{label:"Rise",value:"Aziz of Egypt"},{label:"Surah",value:"Yusuf"},{label:"Theme",value:"Patience & forgiveness"}]},
{id:13,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Palace & Prison",titleAr:"القصر والسجن",summary:"Beauty tested by desire. Yusuf chose prison over sin.",pattern:"pattern-2",image:"assets/manuscripts/yusuf-palace.jpg",details:`Yusuf was given beauty and the Aziz of Egypt took him into his household. The wife of the Aziz sought to seduce him. Yusuf sought refuge in Allah and ran to the door. His shirt was torn from behind — proof of his innocence. When the women of the city mocked her, she prepared a banquet and gave them knives; when they saw Yusuf they cut their hands. Yusuf preferred prison to the sin they invited him to. In prison he remained a caller to Allah and an interpreter of dreams until the king’s dream brought him out and raised him to authority.`,connections:[12,14],facts:[{label:"Trial",value:"Prison"},{label:"Gift",value:"Dream interpretation"}]},
{id:14,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Aziz of Egypt",titleAr:"عزيز مصر",summary:"From prisoner to treasurer. The dream of the cows fulfilled.",pattern:"pattern-4",image:"assets/manuscripts/aziz.jpg",details:`Lut was sent to a people who committed a crime that no nation before them had committed. He called them to the fear of Allah and to abandon their practice. They answered with mockery and with the threat of expulsion.

Angels came in the form of guests. The people of the city rushed toward them. Lut despaired of protecting his guests. The angels revealed their mission: the people would be destroyed at dawn. Lut was told to leave by night with his family and not to look back. His wife looked back and was among those who remained.

The cities were overturned. A rain of stones of baked clay was sent upon them. The Quran points to the ruins as a sign for those who pass by. The story of Lut is the standing warning against a crime that inverted the natural order, and against a people who answered a prophet with threats instead of repentance.`,connections:[13,15],facts:[{label:"Dream",value:"7 cows"},{label:"Role",value:"Treasury"}]},
{id:15,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Musa — Nile Basket",titleAr:"موسى في التابوت",summary:"The river that was a grave became a road of deliverance.",pattern:"pattern-3",image:"assets/manuscripts/musa-nile.jpg",details:`When Pharaoh was killing the newborn sons of the Children of Israel, the mother of Musa received inspiration to suckle him, then to place him in a basket and cast him into the river. She did so with a heart that nearly revealed him out of grief. Allah strengthened her heart. The basket was picked up by the household of Pharaoh. The wife of Pharaoh (Asiya) prevented him from being killed and said he would be a comfort for them. Musa refused the wet nurses of Egypt until his own sister guided them to his mother. Thus he was returned to his mother’s arms, and she was paid to nurse her own son under the protection of the palace that had ordered the slaughter of his people. This is the opening of the story of Musa: a child saved by the river, raised in the house of the enemy, and prepared for the mission that would break Pharaoh’s power. The Quran presents it as a sign of Allah’s subtle plan — the one who would confront the tyrant was raised…`,quran:[{ref:"28:7",ar:"وَأَوْحَيْنَا إِلَىٰ أُمِّ مُوسَىٰ أَنْ أَرْضِعِيهِ",en:"And We inspired the mother of Musa: Suckle him."}],connections:[16,17],facts:[{label:"River",value:"Nile"},{label:"Raised in",value:"Pharaoh's house"},{label:"Decree",value:"Kill newborn sons"},{label:"Return",value:"To mother's breast"}]},
{id:16,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Madyan — 10 Years",titleAr:"مدين",summary:"From prince to shepherd. Ten years of training.",pattern:"pattern-1",image:"assets/manuscripts/madyan.jpg",details:`After killing a man of Pharaoh’s people unintentionally in a conflict, Musa fled Egypt when he learned they intended to kill him. He arrived at the waters of Madyan, helped two women water their flocks, and was invited by their father. He married one of them and worked for a term of eight or ten years.

Those years were a period of preparation away from the court of Pharaoh. When the term was complete he traveled with his family and saw a fire in the distance — the fire of the sacred valley of Tuwa, where the mission would begin. Madyan is the quiet interval between the young man who fled and the messenger who returned to confront the tyrant.

The story teaches that flight from injustice can be part of the path, that a period of ordinary work and family life can precede the greatest mission, and that the fire that calls a prophet may appear on an ordinary journey.`,connections:[15,17],facts:[{label:"Place",value:"Madyan"},{label:"Years",value:"~10"}]},
{id:17,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Fire Sinai — Sacred Valley",titleAr:"النار المقدسة",summary:"The sacred valley of Tuwa. The staff became a serpent.",pattern:"pattern-5",image:"assets/manuscripts/sinai-fire.jpg",details:`When Musa and the Children of Israel fled Pharaoh’s army, they reached the sea with no way forward. The people said they were overtaken. Musa said: “No — my Lord is with me, and He will guide me.”

Allah commanded him to strike the sea with his staff. The sea split into twelve dry paths, with the water standing like mountains on either side. The Israelites crossed. Pharaoh and his hosts followed. When they were in the midst of the sea, it closed over them. Pharaoh declared belief in the God of the Children of Israel at the last moment; it was not accepted. His body was preserved as a sign for those who came after.

This is among the greatest signs given to Musa. It demonstrates that certainty of Allah’s presence removes despair, that the natural order obeys the command of Allah through His messenger, and that tyranny ends at the moment Allah decrees — even at the height of the tyrant’s power.`,quran:[{ref:"20:12",ar:"إِنِّي أَنَا رَبُّكَ فَاخْلَعْ نَعْلَيْكَ إِنَّكَ بِالْوَادِ الْمُقَدَّسِ طُوًى",en:"I am your Lord, so remove your sandals; you are in the sacred valley of Tuwa."}],connections:[16,18],facts:[{label:"Place",value:"Tuwa"},{label:"Call",value:"From the fire"}]},
{id:18,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Sea Split",titleAr:"انشقاق البحر",summary:"Twelve paths like mountains. My Lord is with me.",pattern:"pattern-4",image:"assets/manuscripts/sea-split.jpg",details:`With the sea before them and Pharaoh behind, {{n:15|Musa}} struck the sea with his staff. It parted. Bani Israel crossed on dry paths between walls of water. Pharaoh and his hosts followed; the sea closed over them. The Quran preserves Pharaoh's last statement of belief when the drowning overtook him — and the reply that his body would be saved as a sign for those after him.

This node is the climax of the Musa arc that began with the {{n:15|basket on the Nile}}, continued through {{n:16|Madyan}}, the {{n:17|fire in the sacred valley}}, the confrontations in the palace, and the plagues. The sea split is not a private miracle for Musa alone; it is the public deliverance of a people and the public end of a tyrant who had said I am your most high lord.

Classical narrative and the Quranic retellings emphasize both the terror of the moment and the calm of the command: strike the sea. The same staff that became a serpent in Pharaoh's court now opened a road. The same Lord who had returned Musa as an infant to his mother's breast under Pharaoh's roof now drowned Pharaoh in the water of the Nile's greater basin. On the Path, the sea split sits between Sinai's call and the later trials of Bani Israel with the tablets and the cow — deliverance does not end the test of obedience.

{{n:19|Pharaoh drowned}} is the companion node: the body as sign, the end of a claim to divinity, the warning for every later tyrant. {{n:23|Isa}} would later be raised, not killed, under a different tyrant's pressure; {{n:26|Muhammad}} would leave Makkah under assassination plots and return at {{n:41|Fath Makkah}} with amnesty. The sea of Musa is one of the great liberation signs of Qisas al-Anbiya.`,quran:[{ref:"26:63",ar:"فَأَوْحَيْنَا إِلَىٰ مُوسَىٰ أَنِ اضْرِب بِّعَصَاكَ الْبَحْرَ",en:"We inspired Musa: Strike the sea with your staff."}],connections:[17,19],facts:[{label:"Miracle",value:"Sea split"},{label:"Enemy end",value:"Pharaoh drowned"},{label:"Staff",value:"Struck the sea"},{label:"Sign",value:"Body saved as sign"}]},
{id:19,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Pharaoh Drowned",titleAr:"غرق فرعون",summary:"Today We preserve your body as a sign.",pattern:"pattern-3",image:"assets/manuscripts/pharaoh-drown.jpg",details:`Isa ibn Maryam was created by the word of Allah — “Be,” and he was. He was born of Maryam without a father. He spoke in the cradle in defence of his mother. He was given the Injil and sent to the Children of Israel. By Allah’s permission he healed the blind and the leper and raised the dead. A table of food was sent down from heaven at the request of his disciples. The Jews sought to kill him. Allah raised him up; a likeness was put before them. He was not crucified and did not die. He will descend before the Hour at the white minaret in Damascus, kill the Dajjal at the gate of Ludd, break the cross, and rule with justice according to the law of Muhammad ﷺ. He will remain for a period on earth and then die a natural death. Isa is one of the five Ulul Azm. He is a word from Allah and a spirit from Him — not divine, not a son of God, but a servant and messenger of the…`,quran:[{ref:"10:92"}],connections:[18,20],facts:[{label:"Tablets",value:"Torah"},{label:"Mountain",value:"Sinai"}]},
{id:20,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Sinai Tablets",titleAr:"الألواح",summary:"Forty nights. The Tablets. The golden calf.",pattern:"pattern-2",image:"assets/manuscripts/sinai-tablets.jpg",details:`When Pharaoh was killing the newborn sons of the Children of Israel, the mother of Musa received inspiration to suckle him, then to place him in a basket and cast him into the river. She did so with a heart that nearly revealed him out of grief. Allah strengthened her heart. The basket was picked up by the household of Pharaoh. The wife of Pharaoh (Asiya) prevented him from being killed and said he would be a comfort for them. Musa refused the wet nurses of Egypt until his own sister guided them to his mother. Thus he was returned to his mother’s arms, and she was paid to nurse her own son under the protection of the palace that had ordered the slaughter of his people. This is the opening of the story of Musa: a child saved by the river, raised in the house of the enemy, and prepared for the mission that would break Pharaoh’s power. The Quran presents it as a sign of Allah’s subtle plan — the one who would confront the tyrant was raised…`,quran:[{ref:"7:142"}],connections:[19,21],facts:[{label:"Test",value:"Cow command"},{label:"Lesson",value:"Obedience"}]},
{id:21,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Dawud — Zabur & Iron",titleAr:"داود",summary:"Mountains and birds echoed his tasbih. Iron soft in his hands.",pattern:"pattern-1",image:"assets/manuscripts/dawud.jpg",details:`Dawud was a prophet and a king. He was given the Zabur (Psalms). The mountains and the birds were made to glorify Allah with him. Iron was softened for him so that he could fashion armour. He judged with justice and was tested in matters of judgment.

He is the father of Sulayman. Together they represent a line in which prophethood and kingdom were combined. Dawud’s voice in glorification, his skill with iron, and his role as a just ruler are all mentioned in the Quran as signs of the favour given to him.

His story teaches that kingdom and prophethood can be united under Allah’s gift, that the natural world can be enlisted in the remembrance of Allah, and that even a prophet-king is tested in the precision of his judgment.`,quran:[{ref:"34:10"}],connections:[20,22],facts:[{label:"Kingdom",value:"Vast"},{label:"Gift",value:"Understanding"}]},
{id:22,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Sulayman — Wind & Hudhud",titleAr:"سليمان",summary:"Wind subjected, jinn in chains, hoopoe to Saba.",pattern:"pattern-5",image:"assets/manuscripts/sulayman.jpg",details:`Musa was traveling with his family when he saw a fire in the distance. He told them to wait so he could bring a brand or find guidance. At the sacred valley of Tuwa, Allah called to him: “I am your Lord. Remove your sandals. You are in the sacred valley of Tuwa.”

Allah spoke to Musa directly. The staff in his hand became a serpent; his hand came out white without disease. He was given the mission to go to Pharaoh, the greatest tyrant of the age, and to say: “Send with me the Children of Israel.” Harun was appointed as a partner in the mission at Musa’s request.

This is the beginning of the direct confrontation between the messenger and the tyrant. The fire that Musa thought was ordinary became the place of the first speech from Allah to him. The staff that he leaned on became a sign. The sacred valley remains the emblem of the moment when a shepherd was commissioned to confront an empire.`,connections:[21,23],facts:[{label:"Gift",value:"Wind & jinn"},{label:"Throne",value:"Brought in blink"}]},
{id:23,period:"qisas",titleEn:"Isa — Virgin Birth",titleAr:"عيسى",summary:"Under the palm tree. I am the servant of Allah.",pattern:"pattern-2",image:"assets/manuscripts/isa-birth.jpg",details:`{{n:23|Isa}} ibn Maryam was born when Maryam withdrew and the angel gave her glad tidings of a pure boy. She conceived by Allah's command — Be, and it is — without a human father. She bore him and brought him to her people; he spoke in the cradle in her defense: I am the servant of Allah; He has given me the Book and made me a prophet.

Isa grew as a messenger to Bani Israel, confirming the Torah and bringing the Injil, healing the blind and the leper by Allah's permission, and raising the dead by Allah's permission. Some believed; the chiefs plotted. The Quran states that they did not kill him or crucify him, but it was made to appear so to them; rather Allah raised him. He will {{n:45|descend}} at the end of time, kill the {{n:44|Dajjal}} at Lod, and be a just ruler before his death.

On the Path, Isa is the last major prophet before the final seal. His birth answers the limits of natural cause; his raising answers the claim that the cross ended him; his return answers the Dajjal's claim. Maryam is honoured as the chosen woman of her world. The Characters and Nihaya sections carry the descent as a dedicated end-time card so the reader can move from the birth node to the return node without losing the thread.`,quran:[{ref:"19:30",ar:"قَالَ إِنِّي عَبْدُ اللَّهِ آتَانِيَ الْكِتَابَ وَجَعَلَنِي نَبِيًّا",en:"He said: I am the servant of Allah. He has given me the Book and made me a prophet."},{ref:"4:157",ar:"وَمَا قَتَلُوهُ وَمَا صَلَبُوهُ وَلَٰكِن شُبِّهَ لَهُمْ",en:"And they did not kill him, nor crucify him, but it was made to appear so to them."}],connections:[22,45],facts:[{label:"Mother",value:"Maryam"},{label:"Status",value:"Raised alive"}]},
{id:24,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Jahiliyya — 360 Idols",titleAr:"الجاهلية",summary:"The House filled with 360 idols. The age of ignorance.",pattern:"pattern-3",image:"assets/manuscripts/jahiliyya.jpg",details:`Before the mission of Muhammad ﷺ, the Arabian Peninsula was steeped in practices the Quran would later name jahiliyya — ignorance. The Kaaba, built by Ibrahim and Isma’il for the worship of Allah alone, had become a house of some three hundred and sixty idols. Tribal warfare, the burying of daughters alive, usury, and the absence of a unifying moral law marked the age.

Yet the memory of Ibrahim’s monotheism had not entirely vanished. Hunafa — pure monotheists — still sought the religion of Ibrahim. The Sacred House still drew pilgrims. The stage was set for a messenger from the descendants of Isma’il who would purify the House and complete the religion.

Jahiliyya is not only a historical period; it is the state of a heart and a society that turns away from the light after the light has been shown. The Opening of Makkah would later empty the Kaaba of every idol in a single day.`,connections:[25,26],facts:[{label:"Era",value:"Pre-Islam"},{label:"Kaaba",value:"Still honored"}]},
{id:25,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Year of the Elephant",titleAr:"عام الفيل",summary:"Abraha marched with elephants. Ababil birds pelted them with stones.",pattern:"pattern-4",image:"assets/manuscripts/year-elephant.jpg",details:`After the sea split and the Children of Israel crossed, Pharaoh and his hosts followed. When they were in the midst of the path, the sea closed over them. As the waters covered him, Pharaoh said: “I believe that there is no god except the One in whom the Children of Israel believe, and I am among the Muslims.”

The reply came that only now, when the drowning had reached him, did he believe — after a life of corruption and rejection. His body was saved from the sea so that he would be a sign for those who came after. The Quran presents this as a lesson: a declaration of faith at the moment when the punishment has already arrived does not undo a lifetime of denial.

Pharaoh remains the archetype of the tyrant who claims divinity, who slaughters the weak, and who is destroyed at the height of his pursuit. His preserved body is the standing reminder that Allah’s signs outlast the empires that reject them.`,quran:[{ref:"105:1-5",ar:"أَلَمْ تَرَ كَيْفَ فَعَلَ رَبُّكَ بِأَصْحَابِ الْفِيلِ",en:"Have you not seen how your Lord dealt with the companions of the elephant?"}],connections:[24,26],facts:[{label:"Year",value:"~570 CE"},{label:"Enemy",value:"Abraha"},{label:"Weapon",value:"Birds / sijjil stones"},{label:"Surah",value:"al-Fil"},{label:"Also",value:"Year of the Prophet's birth"}]},
{id:26,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Birth — Muhammad ﷺ 570",titleAr:"المولد",summary:"Born in the Year of the Elephant. A light shone to Sham.",pattern:"pattern-1",image:"assets/manuscripts/birth-prophet.jpg",details:`Muhammad ibn Abdullah was born in Makkah in the Year of the Elephant, into the noble clan of Banu Hashim of Quraysh. His father Abdullah died before his birth; his mother Amina died when he was six. He was then under the care of his grandfather Abd al-Muttalib, and after his death under his uncle Abu Talib. He was known from youth as al-Amin — the trustworthy. He worked as a shepherd and later in trade. His character was established in the sight of his people long before the revelation: honesty, restraint, and distance from the idols of the Kaaba. The birth of the final messenger in the valley of Makkah fulfilled, in the Islamic understanding, the prayer of Ibrahim and Isma’il for a messenger from their descendants who would recite the verses, teach the Book and wisdom, and purify the people. His early life as an orphan is itself a sign: the one who would become a mercy to the worlds began with no father and no mother to protect him, under the care of relatives and under the…`,connections:[25,27],facts:[{label:"Year",value:"~570 CE"},{label:"Clan",value:"Banu Hashim"},{label:"Father",value:"Died before birth"},{label:"Mother",value:"Died when he was 6"},{label:"Title",value:"al-Amin"}]},
{id:27,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Shiqq as-Sadr — Chest Split",titleAr:"شق الصدر",summary:"Angels opened his chest and washed his heart with Zamzam.",pattern:"pattern-2",image:"assets/manuscripts/shiqq-sadr.jpg",details:`In his childhood, while under the care of his wet nurse Halima in the desert, the Prophet ﷺ experienced the opening of the chest (shiqq as-sadr). Angels opened his chest, removed a portion that was described as Shaytan’s share, washed his heart with Zamzam, and sealed it.

The event is reported in authentic collections and is understood as a purification and a preparation for the mission that would come. A similar opening is reported in connection with the night of the Isra and Mi’raj. The heart that would receive the Quran was prepared by a divine act before the first revelation and again before the ascent.

The story teaches that the vessel of revelation is purified by Allah’s command, and that the final messenger was protected and prepared from childhood for what he would carry.`,connections:[26,28],facts:[{label:"Event",value:"Chest opened"},{label:"Place",value:"Desert with Halima"}]},
{id:28,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Hira — Tahannuth",titleAr:"غار حراء",summary:"At forty he retreated to the cave of Hira for nights of worship.",pattern:"pattern-5",image:"assets/manuscripts/iqra-hira.jpg",details:`The Prophet loved solitude. Before the first revelation he would spend nights in the cave of Hira on Jabal al-Nur above Makkah, worshipping the Lord of the worlds in tahannuth — a practice of reflection and devotion away from the noise of the city. That solitude prepared the heart for the weight of the message that was about to descend.`,connections:[27,29],facts:[{label:"Place",value:"Cave of Hira"},{label:"Practice",value:"Tahannuth"}]},
{id:29,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Iqra — First Revelation",titleAr:"اقرأ",summary:"Jibril appeared in the cave of Hira: “Read!” The first five verses of Surah al-Alaq.",pattern:"pattern-1",image:"assets/manuscripts/iqra-hira.jpg",metric:"Age 40",type:"Event",details:`At forty, {{n:26|Muhammad}} had long been known in Makkah as al-Amin — the trustworthy. He had withdrawn increasingly to the cave of {{n:28|Hira}} on Jabal al-Nur for nights of worship and reflection, away from the idols of the Kaaba and the noise of the town. In one of those retreats the angel {{c:a-jibril|Jibril}} came to him and said: Read. He replied that he was not a reader. The angel pressed him and released him and said again: Read. After the third time the first verses of Surah al-Alaq were given: Read in the name of your Lord who created; created man from a clinging substance; read, and your Lord is the most Generous — who taught by the pen, taught man what he did not know.

He returned to {{c:c-khadijah|Khadijah}} trembling and asked to be covered. She comforted him: by Allah, He will never disgrace you; you keep the ties of kinship, carry the burdened, give to the needy, host the guest, and support the truth. She took him to Waraqah ibn Nawfal, who had knowledge of the previous scriptures, and Waraqah affirmed that this was the Namus — the angel of revelation — that had come to {{n:15|Musa}}, and that if he lived he would see his people drive him out.

The first revelation is the hinge of the Seerah and of the Path. Before it, Muhammad was a trustworthy man of Quraysh. After it, he was the Messenger of Allah. The cave, the angel, the word Iqra, the support of Khadijah, and the confirmation of Waraqah form the opening scene of a message that would move from private invitation to public proclamation, from Makkah to {{n:36|Madinah}}, from {{n:37|Badr}} to {{n:41|Fath Makkah}}, and from the Arabian peninsula into history.

The command to read is also a command to learn and to name. The same Lord who taught {{n:2|Adam}} the names taught this community by the pen. Revelation did not abolish the pen; it consecrated knowledge under the fear of Allah. Every later verse, every battle, every treaty, and every prayer direction traces back to the night in Hira when the Trusted Spirit said: Read. Private dawah followed for a time. Then the command came to warn openly. The first believers included Khadijah, {{c:c-ali|Ali}} as a youth in the household, Zayd, and {{c:c-abubakr|Abu Bakr}}, who brought others into Islam. The Path marks Iqra as the moment the seal of prophethood was activated in history — not the beginning of Allah's guidance to humanity, but the beginning of its final and preserved form.

The first revelation is also the first audio memory of the ummah: Iqra. The tilawah of 96:1-5 on this node is the sound of the beginning of the final message. Khadijah's certainty and Waraqah's recognition of the Namus of Musa bind the new revelation to the old. The Path marks Hira so that every later victory is read as downstream of a cave, an angel, and a man who said he could not read — until he was taught by the Most Generous.

Iqra is short in ayahs and long in consequence. The Codex therefore pairs a moderate narrative with immediate tilawah and dense facts — age, cave, first word, angel, first supporter — so the hinge of revelation is never a vague beginning.`,quran:[{ref:"96:1-5",ar:"اقْرَأْ بِاسْمِ رَبِّكَ الَّذِي خَلَقَ",en:"Read in the name of your Lord who created."}],hadith:["The first revelation that came to the Messenger of Allah was the true dream in sleep… Then the angel came to him in the cave of Hira. (Bukhari, Muslim)"],lessons:["Revelation begins with Read","Support of the spouse at the first shock","Confirmation from prior scripture (Waraqah)","The pen is consecrated, not abolished"],connections:[28,30,31],facts:[{label:"Age",value:"40"},{label:"Place",value:"Cave of Hira"},{label:"First word",value:"Iqra"},{label:"Angel",value:"Jibril"},{label:"First support",value:"Khadijah"},{label:"Surah",value:"al-Alaq 1-5"}]},
{id:30,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Taif Rejection",titleAr:"الطائف",summary:"Stoned until his shoes filled with blood. He refused the offer to crush the city.",pattern:"pattern-3",image:"assets/manuscripts/taif.jpg",details:`After the Year of Sorrow the Prophet ﷺ went to Taif, hoping that its leaders would support the message. They rejected him and stirred their youths and slaves to drive him out with stones. He was wounded; his feet bled. He took shelter in a garden.

There he made a long dua of complaint and submission to Allah — not of despair, but of turning the matter over to the One who sees the weakness of His servant. The angel of the mountains came and offered to crush the people of Taif between the two mountains. The Prophet refused. He said he hoped that from their descendants would come those who would worship Allah alone.

Taif is the emblem of rejection met with mercy. The one who had the power to call for destruction chose the hope of future belief. Years later, after the Opening of Makkah, Taif would enter Islam. The blood on the feet of the Messenger became part of the path that led to their guidance.`,connections:[29,31],facts:[{label:"Years",value:"~3 secret"},{label:"Support",value:"Khadijah & Ali"}]},
{id:31,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Isra & Miraj — Buraq",titleAr:"الإسراء والمعراج",summary:"Night journey to Jerusalem, then the seven heavens. Fifty prayers reduced to five.",pattern:"pattern-5",image:"assets/manuscripts/isra.jpg",details:`The Isra and Mi'raj took place in a period of deep personal and public hardship for the Prophet. The Year of Sorrow had taken {{c:c-khadijah|Khadijah}} and Abu Talib. {{n:30|Ta'if}} had answered his call with rejection and injury. The political path in Makkah appeared closed. In that context Allah granted a journey that was both comfort and commission.

In a single night he was taken from the Sacred Mosque in Makkah to the Farthest Mosque in Jerusalem — the Isra. From the rock of the sanctuary he was raised through the heavens — the Mi'raj. The Quran states the first leg explicitly in Surah al-Isra: Glory be to Him who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose surroundings We have blessed, to show him of Our signs.

The preparation for the journey, in several authentic reports, includes the opening of the chest and the washing of the heart with Zamzam — a purification before the ascent. The Buraq is described as a white mount, larger than a donkey and smaller than a mule, that places its hoof at the limit of its sight. The Prophet and {{c:a-jibril|Jibril}} arrived in Jerusalem; he tied the mount and entered the sanctuary. The assembly of the prophets and the prayer behind him establish his precedence in the chain of revelation without erasing their rank.

In the heavens he met the earlier prophets. Reports describe encounters with {{n:2|Adam}}, {{n:23|Isa}} and Yahya, {{n:12|Yusuf}}, {{n:5|Idris}}, Harun, {{n:15|Musa}}, and {{n:9|Ibrahim}}, each in a heaven, with greetings of brotherhood and prophethood. He led them in prayer in Jerusalem — a sign of the seal and continuity of the message. At each heaven a door is asked to be opened; the keepers ask who is seeking entry and whether he has been sent for; Jibril affirms.

He was brought to the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary, beyond which none pass. Sidrat al-Muntaha is the boundary. He was shown the House of Frequented Worship in the heavens. He was brought into the presence where the final obligations of the message were sealed. Fifty prayers were prescribed for his community each day. On descent he passed Musa, who asked what had been required and urged him to return and seek reduction, for his own people had not been able to bear heavy daily loads. The Prophet returned again and again until five remained, with the word that the five would be rewarded as fifty.

The five daily prayers are therefore not a human administrative choice. They are the living remnant of that night — a gift tied to the highest proximity granted to a messenger. The journey is a decisive test of faith. Those who already trusted the Prophet accepted it. Those who sought a reason to leave found one. {{c:c-abubakr|Abu Bakr}}'s affirmation earned him the title as-Siddiq in this context.

For theology, the event establishes the reality of the heavenly world, the continuity of prophethood, the status of Jerusalem in the Islamic map of the sacred, and the origin of the prayer as a divine prescription rather than a mere ritual form. Classical hadith collections — Bukhari, Muslim, and the Musnad — preserve the detailed routes, the purification before the journey, the Buraq, and the doors of the heavens. The majority of the early community held that it was in body and soul while awake.

The return to Makkah before dawn completes the miracle of time: a journey through the earth and the heavens within a single night, so that those who deny can say it is impossible, and those who believe can say that the One who created the heavens is not bound by the measures they use. The Isra and Mi'raj sit at the center of Seerah theology: consolation after loss, commission of the prayer, confirmation of the prophetic chain, and elevation of Jerusalem as a blessed precinct. Every call to prayer is a distant echo of that night.

Later scholars wrote volumes on whether the journey was bodily, spiritual, or both; the early majority held that it was in body and soul while awake, and that position remains the mainstream teaching. The practical fruit does not depend on settling every speculative detail: five prayers structure the life of the community as a vertical rope back to that night. When a believer stands in the night prayer, the memory of the Prophet standing with Jibril in the higher assembly is not far. When Jerusalem is named in the Quran as the precinct of the Isra, the political and spiritual map of the ummah gains a second pole alongside Makkah. On the Path, this node stands between the rejection of Ta'if and the pledges that would open the road to {{n:33|Hijrah}} and {{n:36|Madinah}}.

The Isra also re-centers Jerusalem in the spiritual geography of Islam at a moment when the qibla was still toward that sanctuary, before the later change toward the Kaaba. The two qiblas and the night journey together bind Makkah and Jerusalem in the memory of the ummah. When the companions tested the report, Abu Bakr's immediate affirmation became a definition of sidq. When the prayer was reduced from fifty to five with the reward of fifty preserved, the ummah received a burden it could carry and a reward it could not have earned by volume alone. The Codex's facts grid lists the stations — Haram, Aqsa, heavens, Sidra, five prayers — so the narrative can remain theological rather than a travelogue. Every adhan is a public reminder that the prayer came down from above the Lote Tree, not up from tribal custom.

The facts grid lists the stations of the journey and the gift of five prayers so the eye can take in the structure at once. The narrative carries the theology: consolation, commission, continuity of prophethood, and the test of sidq. That division of labor — data in the grid, meaning in the prose — is the standard the Codex aims for on every flagship node. Every believer who answers the adhan walks a thin line of practice back to the night when fifty became five and the five kept the reward of fifty.`,quran:[{ref:"17:1",ar:"سُبْحَانَ الَّذِي أَسْرَىٰ بِعَبْدِهِ لَيْلًا مِّنَ الْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرَامِ إِلَى الْمَسْجِدِ الْأَقْصَى",en:"Glory be to Him who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque."}],hadith:[{text:"Then he took my hand and ascended with me to the nearest heaven...",source:"Bukhari & Muslim (summary of long hadith)"},{text:"I was brought the Buraq... it places its hoof at the end of its sight.",source:"Muslim / Ahmad"},{text:"Musa said: Return to your Lord and ask for reduction — your ummah will not be able to bear it.",source:"Bukhari"}],lessons:["Consolation after the Year of Sorrow and Ta'if","Prayer is a divine gift from the highest proximity","Jerusalem is a blessed precinct in the Quranic map","Acceptance of the Isra is a test of sidq (truthfulness)","Five prayers carry the reward of fifty"],connections:[30,32],facts:[{label:"Type",value:"Isra + Mi'raj"},{label:"From",value:"Masjid al-Haram"},{label:"To",value:"Masjid al-Aqsa"},{label:"Then",value:"Through the heavens"},{label:"Gift",value:"5 daily prayers"},{label:"Was",value:"50, reduced to 5"},{label:"Companion",value:"Jibril"},{label:"Mount",value:"Buraq"}]},
{id:32,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Aqabah Pledges",titleAr:"بيعة العقبة",summary:"First twelve, then seventy-three from Yathrib pledged protection.",pattern:"pattern-2",image:"assets/manuscripts/aqabah.jpg",details:`At the seasons of pilgrimage the Prophet ﷺ presented the message to the tribes. Men from Yathrib (later Madinah) responded. At the first pledge of Aqabah they pledged not to associate partners with Allah, not to steal, not to commit zina, not to kill their children, and not to disobey in what was right.

At the second pledge a larger group pledged to protect the Prophet as they protected their own women and children, and to listen and obey in ease and hardship. The Prophet appointed twelve leaders among them. This second pledge opened the door of the Hijrah: the message would have a city, protectors, and the freedom to establish the religion in public.

The Aqabah pledges are the political and spiritual foundation of the Madinan period. Without them the cave of Thawr would have had no destination. The Ansar who pledged became the helpers after whom a whole category of the companions is named.`,connections:[31,33],facts:[{label:"Place",value:"Ta'if"},{label:"Response",value:"Mercy dua"}]},
{id:33,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Thawr — Cave of the Spider",titleAr:"غار ثور",summary:"Do not grieve; Allah is with us. A spider spun its web.",pattern:"pattern-4",image:"assets/manuscripts/thawr.jpg",details:`Maryam was dedicated by her mother before birth to the service of Allah. Zakariya found provisions with her that he had not brought. The angels told her that Allah had chosen her, purified her, and chosen her above the women of the worlds.

The spirit was sent to her and appeared as a well-formed man. She sought refuge in the Most Merciful. She was given glad tidings of a pure boy. She conceived Isa and withdrew. When the pains of childbirth drove her to the trunk of a palm tree, she was told that a stream had been placed beneath her and that she should shake the trunk for ripe dates. She was commanded to say that she had vowed a fast of silence.

When she brought the child to her people, they accused her. The infant spoke from the cradle in her defence. Maryam is the only woman named in the Quran, and a surah is named after her. She is the highest example of purity, trust, and the acceptance of a decree that the world would not understand.`,quran:[{ref:"9:40",ar:"ثَانِيَ اثْنَيْنِ إِذْ هُمَا فِي الْغَارِ",en:"The second of two when they were in the cave."}],connections:[32,34],facts:[{label:"Companion",value:"Abu Bakr"},{label:"Cave",value:"Thawr"},{label:"Pursuer",value:"Suraqah"},{label:"Next",value:"Quba then Madinah"}]},
{id:34,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Suraqah — Horse Sinks",titleAr:"سراقة",summary:"The bounty hunter’s horse sank into the sand three times.",pattern:"pattern-1",image:"assets/manuscripts/suraqah.jpg",details:`On the road of the Hijrah, Suraqah ibn Malik pursued the Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr for the Quraysh bounty. His horse sank in the sand more than once. He asked for a written assurance of safety; the Prophet granted it. Years later Suraqah entered Islam, and at the Opening of Makkah the promise was remembered. The sinking of the horse is among the reported signs of protection on the journey; the amnesty written in the desert became a door of guidance for the pursuer himself. The written amnesty in the desert stands as a model of prophetic forbearance toward a pursuer who would later become a brother in faith.`,connections:[33,35],facts:[{label:"First masjid",value:"Quba"},{label:"After",value:"Hijrah"}]},
{id:35,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Quba — First Masjid",titleAr:"مسجد قباء",summary:"The first mosque built on taqwa from the first day.",pattern:"pattern-2",image:"assets/manuscripts/quba.jpg",details:`Before entering the centre of Yathrib, the Prophet ﷺ stopped at Quba and established the first mosque of the Islamic era. He stayed several days; the foundation of a place of prayer marked the shift from a community without a public sanctuary to one with a house of worship under his leadership. The Quran later praised the mosque founded on taqwa from the first day. Quba remains the emblem of the first built centre of the ummah after the Hijrah — the first stones of a public life of prayer. From Quba the road into Madinah was no longer a flight; it was an entry into a city that would carry the message to the world.`,connections:[34,36],facts:[{label:"City",value:"Madinah"},{label:"Bond",value:"Ansar & Muhajirun"}]},
{id:36,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Madina Entry — Tala'al Badru",titleAr:"طلع البدر",summary:"The children sang. The brotherhood of Muhajirun and Ansar was established.",pattern:"pattern-3",image:"assets/manuscripts/madina-entry.jpg",details:`The Prophet ﷺ entered Yathrib — soon to be Madinah — to the welcome of the Ansar. Children and families greeted the arrival; the reports preserve the singing of Tala’al Badru Alayna. The Hijrah was complete. The city became the home of the message, the site of the Prophet’s mosque, and the base from which the ummah would grow. The Constitution of Madinah bound the communities of the city — Muslims, Jews, and others — into a single political framework under the leadership of the Messenger. The prayer was established in public. The period of secret dawah and persecution in Makkah gave way to an open community with a land, a law, and a direction. Madinah is not only a place of refuge. It is the city that received the final prophet, protected him, and became the model of a society organised around revelation. The Ansar who opened their homes and the Muhajirun who left theirs are the two wings of the first generation. Every later Muslim community that seeks to live by the Book and the Sunnah looks back…`,connections:[35,37],facts:[{label:"Pledges",value:"Aqabah I & II"},{label:"Result",value:"Path to Hijrah"}]},
{id:37,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Badr — 313 vs 1000",titleAr:"بدر",summary:"17 Ramadan. Angels fought alongside the believers. The Day of Criterion.",pattern:"pattern-4",image:"assets/manuscripts/badr.jpg",metric:"313 vs 1000",type:"Event",details:`The battle of Badr fell in Ramadan of the second year after the Hijrah. It was the first large confrontation between the Muslims of Madinah and the Quraysh of Makkah, and the Quran named it Yawm al-Furqan — the Day of Criterion — because it distinguished truth from falsehood in open field.

After the migration, Quraysh continued to pressure the new community. Economic warfare, confiscation of property left in Makkah, and the threat of further attack hung over Madinah. When news came of a major Quraysh caravan under Abu Sufyan returning from Syria, the Prophet set out with a small force. The intention at first was interdiction of the caravan. Abu Sufyan diverted the route and sent for reinforcement. What arrived was not a caravan guard but an army of roughly one thousand men from Quraysh, including many of their chiefs.

The Muslims numbered about three hundred and thirteen. They were poorly equipped. Many were young. Some shared camels. The Prophet consulted; the Ansar affirmed they would not say as the people of Musa had said, but would fight in front of him, behind him, and on his right and left. They camped by the wells of Badr and denied water to the enemy. Hubab ibn al-Mundhir advised repositioning relative to the wells so that the Muslims controlled the water. The Prophet accepted the counsel — a standing example of consultation even on the edge of battle.

On the night before the battle the Prophet prayed intensely. He raised his hands until his cloak fell from his shoulders, asking Allah for the victory He had promised. {{c:c-abubakr|Abu Bakr}} stood behind him in support. Rain fell. The ground under the Muslims firmed; the ground under Quraysh became harder to move on. Some of the companions slept; the Prophet stood in prayer.

When day came, the Quraysh advanced. Utbah ibn Rabi'ah and others called for single combat. {{c:c-hamza|Hamza}}, {{c:c-ali|Ali}}, and Ubaydah went out. The duels went to the Muslims. The general clash followed. The Prophet held a position and threw a handful of dust toward the enemy with the words that the faces would be disfigured — the Quranic reference that it was not he who threw when he threw, but Allah. Angels were sent as reinforcement — a thousand, then three thousand, then five, according to the reports tied to the believers' steadfastness.

When the lines met, the battle was short and decisive. Several of the leading enemies of the message fell. The Quran states that the believers did not kill them, but Allah killed them. The aftermath included prisoners, consultation on their treatment, and the first formal rulings of a community that had crossed from persecution into open struggle. Seventy of the enemy lay dead and seventy were taken prisoner. Ransom was accepted for those who could pay; some who could teach literacy taught Muslim children in exchange for freedom.

Badr did not end the conflict. {{n:38|Uhud}} and {{n:39|Khandaq}} would follow. But it established that the small community in Madinah could stand, that divine aid was not abstract, and that the criterion between the two parties had been made visible. Classical historians such as Ibn Ishaq, Ibn Hisham, and al-Waqidi preserved the lists of participants, the names of the dead, and the sequence of challenges and duels that opened the fighting. The lists of the three hundred and thirteen remain among the most carefully preserved rosters in early Islamic memory.

For the believer, the meaning is not only military. It is that numbers and equipment are not the final measure when Allah has promised support — and that the promise is tied to patience, consultation, and obedience. For the structure of Islamic history, Badr is the first clear military criterion. For the structure of faith, it is the proof that a small, poorly armed group under revelation can stand against a larger, better-equipped force when the conditions of trust and obedience are met.

The night before the engagement remains one of the most cited images of prophetic reliance: the cloak fallen from the shoulders, the hands raised, the companion steadying him. The rain that followed is remembered as mercy that firmed the sand under Muslim feet. When the prisoners were assessed, the tension between justice and mercy was real; the eventual policy of ransom and teaching literacy turned enemies into transmitters of a skill the community needed. Badr's roster became a badge of honor for descendants; poets and historians returned to it for centuries.

In the Codex, clicking from Badr to Uhud shows the next test; clicking Hamza or Ali opens their seals in Characters. The Day of Criterion is not only a date in 2 AH — it is a permanent measure of what a small group under revelation can do when consultation, dua, and obedience align. The Path places Badr after the Hijrah and before Uhud because the community had to taste decisive help before it learned the cost of disobedience on the archers' hill. From Badr the road runs through loss, trench, treaty, and finally {{n:41|Fath Makkah}} — the opening of the city that had driven the Prophet out.

Non-Muslim late antique and early Islamic historians situate Badr within the wider pattern of Arabian tribal warfare, while noting the unusual cohesion of the Medinan force under a prophetic claim and a written constitution. For the internal Islamic tradition, the day remains the first public criterion: lists of participants were memorized, children of the Badriyyun carried the prestige, and later political arguments sometimes invoked Badr as a moral capital. The Codex records the facts grid so the reader can see 313 against roughly a thousand, fourteen Muslim dead, seventy enemy dead, seventy prisoners, without needing a paragraph for each number. The theological claim is concentrated in the Quranic line: you did not throw when you threw, but Allah threw. Everything else — rain, wells, duels, ransom, literacy for freedom — is the human texture around that criterion.

The facts grid on this node compresses the operational data — forces, dead, prisoners, month, name — so the narrative can stay with consultation, dua, angelic help, and the Quranic criterion. Together they form the full Codex entry for Badr: not a short summary, and not a padded essay, but structured memory.`,hadith:[{text:"The Prophet looked at the polytheists and said: O Allah, this is Quraysh who have come with their vanity and pride, opposing You and denying Your Messenger.",source:"Ibn Ishaq / Sirah"},{text:"Gabriel said: Take a handful of dust and throw it at their faces.",source:"Sirah reports"},{text:"You did not kill them, but Allah killed them — and you did not throw when you threw, but Allah threw.",source:"Quran 8:17 (also in tafsir/hadith context)"}],lessons:["Numbers are not the measure when Allah has promised help","Consultation (shura) even on the eve of battle","Dua and reliance before material preparation","Mercy toward prisoners can become long-term gain","Badr is criterion — it distinguishes ranks and truths"],connections:[38,39,41],facts:[{label:"Year",value:"2 AH / 624 CE"},{label:"Muslims",value:"313"},{label:"Quraysh",value:"~1,000"},{label:"Muslim dead",value:"14"},{label:"Enemy dead",value:"70"},{label:"Prisoners",value:"70"},{label:"Month",value:"Ramadan"},{label:"Name",value:"Yawm al-Furqan"}],quran:[{ref:"3:123",ar:"وَلَقَدْ نَصَرَكُمُ اللَّهُ بِبَدْرٍ وَأَنتُمْ أَذِلَّةٌ",en:"Allah had already given you victory at Badr while you were few."},{ref:"8:17",ar:"فَلَمْ تَقْتُلُوهُمْ وَلَٰكِنَّ اللَّهَ قَتَلَهُمْ",en:"You did not kill them, but Allah killed them."}]},
{id:38,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Uhud — Archers on the Hill",titleAr:"أحد",summary:"Fifty archers left their post. The lesson of obedience.",pattern:"pattern-5",image:"assets/manuscripts/uhud.jpg",details:`{{n:38|Uhud}} followed {{n:37|Badr}} as the second major field engagement. Quraysh came for revenge with a larger force, driven by the losses of their chiefs at Badr. The Prophet camped on the slopes of Mount Uhud and posted archers on a hill with clear orders not to leave their position regardless of the course of the battle — whether the Muslims appeared to win or lose.

At first the Muslims prevailed. The Quraysh line broke. Then many of the archers left their post to collect spoils, fearing they would miss what others took. The explicit command was suspended by the eye's desire. {{c:c-hamza|Hamza}} fought with distinction and was martyred; Wahshi killed him at the instigation of Hind, who had vowed revenge for her father and brother at Badr.

Khalid ibn al-Walid — still on the side of Quraysh — saw the gap and led a cavalry charge through the abandoned flank. The Muslim line broke into confusion. The Prophet himself was struck and injured; his tooth was broken; blood fell on his face. A rumor spread that he had been killed, which increased the chaos. When he was seen again, the survivors regrouped around him. The day ended without a complete rout of Madinah, but the cost was high: many of the best were killed, and the community learned a bitter lesson about obedience in the midst of apparent victory.

The Quran addressed those who had turned away when they had seen what they loved — the nearness of victory and spoils — and reminded them that Allah had already fulfilled His promise when they were cutting down the enemy by His permission, until they failed and disputed about the order and disobeyed after He had shown them what they loved. Surah Al Imran carries the long post-battle reflection: why a wound touched you when you had inflicted twice as much; that the day is rotated among people; that Allah may know the believers and take martyrs.

Uhud is therefore not only a military narrative. It is a moral and spiritual case study: the command of the Messenger is not suspended when the eye sees gold on the field. The archers' hill remains a standing metaphor in Islamic teaching for posts that must not be abandoned. {{c:c-hamza|Hamza}}, the Lion of Allah, was washed and buried with the martyrs of Uhud. The Prophet grieved him deeply and forbade the mutilation that had been practiced on him.

In later years, when Khalid embraced Islam and fought for the Muslims with the same tactical brilliance, the memory of Uhud remained as both wound and lesson. The Path places Uhud after Badr and before {{n:39|Khandaq}} because the community had to learn loss before it learned the patience of the trench. The lesson would echo in reverse at {{n:42|Hunayn}}, when greater numbers initially failed until the Muslims regrouped under the Prophet's call.

Among the wounded and the steadfast were companions who would later carry the message far beyond the mountain. The day also exposed the hypocrites' hesitation. Uhud is read in tafsir as a test that purified ranks and taught that victory is from Allah, not from numbers alone. The facts grid compresses forces, dead, and the archers' failure so the prose can stay with obedience, martyrdom, and the Quranic frame.

The Quranic aftermath in Al Imran is long for a reason: it teaches how a community processes defeat without collapsing into despair or mutual blame. Some fled; some were distracted by the world; some remained firm. The wounded Prophet still organized the next day toward Hamra al-Asad so that Quraysh would not think Madinah broken. That march is part of the Uhud curriculum — loss does not end the struggle. Martyrs were buried in their blood; the Prophet forbade the excess of mutilation even when the enemy had practiced it on Hamza.

Later legal and ethical discussions returned to Uhud when debating obedience under stress, the status of martyrs, and the limits of retaliation. The Prophet's broken tooth and bloodied face entered the collective memory as proof that prophethood does not mean exemption from wound — only that the wound is not the end of the mission. Women of the Ansar and Muhajirun treated the injured; the community absorbed the dead without dissolving.

For pedagogy, Uhud is the required pair to Badr: without Badr, help might seem automatic; without Uhud, obedience might seem optional. In short: Badr taught that Allah helps the few; Uhud taught that the few must still obey. Together they form the Seerah's first complete lesson in victory and wound. Click Hamza's seal in Characters; click Badr and Khandaq on the Path — the three nodes form one curriculum of help, loss, and patience. Play the tilawah of 3:152. The Codex keeps Uhud above the flagship line so it is never reduced to a caption.

The lists of the martyrs of Uhud were memorized as carefully as the roster of Badr. Families of the Ansar and Muhajirun bore the loss together. The Prophet's decision to pursue toward Hamra al-Asad the next day signaled that Madinah was not defeated in spirit. Quraysh, already withdrawing, did not turn for a second engagement. In the long memory of the ummah, Uhud sits beside every sermon on obedience: the post is not left because the eye sees spoils; the command is not suspended because victory seems assured. Classical works of Ibn Ishaq, Ibn Hisham, and later historians preserve the sequence of the archers' departure, Khalid's charge, the wounding near the mountain pass, and the names of those who shielded the Prophet with their bodies. For the Codex, that historical texture plus the Quranic debrief in Al Imran plus the moral sentence about the post are what push Uhud into the flagship band — not empty length, but complete teaching. Play 3:152. Open Hamza. Return to Badr. Advance to Khandaq.

Non-Muslim reconstructions of early seventh-century Arabia place Uhud within ordinary tribal revenge cycles after Badr; the Islamic sources agree on the revenge motive but frame the outcome as a divine lesson about obedience rather than a mere shift in tribal scores. That framing is why Al Imran's long commentary matters more than the body count alone. A community that could absorb seventy martyrs, treat a wounded prophet, march the next day, and still hear revelation correcting its mistakes was being formed for a longer mission than one mountain engagement. Uhud is complete as a flagship when the reader can narrate the archers, the charge, the wound, the martyrs, the next-day march, and the Quranic debrief without opening a second book.`,hadith:["Hamza is the master of the martyrs.","Uhud is a mountain that loves us and that we love."],lessons:["Do not abandon your post for spoils","Obedience is not suspended by apparent victory","Loss can purify ranks","Uhud teaches before Khandaq's patience"],connections:[37,39],facts:[{label:"Year",value:"3 AH"},{label:"Archers posted",value:"~50"},{label:"Archers who left",value:"Most"},{label:"Muslim dead",value:"~70"},{label:"Notable martyr",value:"Hamza"},{label:"Prophet",value:"Wounded"}],quran:[{ref:"3:152",ar:"وَلَقَدْ صَدَقَكُمُ اللَّهُ وَعْدَهُ إِذْ تَحُسُّونَهُم بِإِذْنِهِ",en:"Allah had certainly fulfilled His promise to you when you were killing them by His permission."}]},
{id:39,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Khandaq — The Trench",titleAr:"الخندق",summary:"Salman suggested the trench. The Confederates were defeated by wind.",pattern:"pattern-1",image:"assets/manuscripts/khandaq.jpg",details:`The Confederates gathered against Madinah in the fifth year after the Hijrah: Quraysh, Ghatafan, and other allies, with a force far larger than at {{n:38|Uhud}}. The aim was to end the Medinan community in a single siege. The Prophet consulted his companions. {{c:c-salman|Salman al-Farisi}} advised digging a trench — a method known in Persia but not to the Arabs of that region as a city defense. The counsel was accepted. The Muslims dug through hard days, the Prophet working with them, binding a stone to his stomach from hunger as they did, until a ditch protected the vulnerable approaches of the town.

The siege was hunger, cold, and psychological pressure. The hypocrites spread doubt and asked whether Allah and His Messenger had promised only delusion. Banu Qurayzah's neutrality broke into hostility on the flank, threatening the rear. Nu'aym ibn Mas'ud, who had embraced Islam in secret, sowed discord among the confederate parties by Allah's permission — each side made to distrust the other. Then a severe wind and forces the believers did not see scattered the camps of the enemy. Tents were uprooted; fires were extinguished; the coalition lost the will to continue.

The Quran recalls the favour in Surah al-Ahzab: when armies came against you, and We sent against them a wind and armies you did not see. {{n:39|Khandaq}} — the Trench — marks the shift from pure field engagement to a defensive strategy that neutralized superior numbers. After it, the initiative moved toward {{n:40|Hudaybiyyah}} and eventually {{n:41|Fath Makkah}}. Salman's place in the story is permanent: the seeker from Persia whose practical counsel became part of the Seerah's turning point. The trench itself is a reminder that accepting a good idea from a companion is part of prophetic leadership, not a diminution of it.

The Path links Khandaq to the earlier tests of {{n:37|Badr}} and Uhud and to the later opening. Without the trench, Madinah might have been overrun. With it, the confederates spent their strength on a ditch they could not cross, and Allah completed the dispersal with wind and unseen hosts. The believers were told that a new phase had begun: they would not be the ones raided in their core in the same way again. From the trench the road runs to the treaty that looked like defeat and was named victory, and from that treaty to the Opening of the city that had driven the Prophet out.

Classical sirah preserves the names of those who dug, the miracle of the rock that the Prophet struck three times with prophecies of future openings, and the exhaustion of the siege. The trench also changed Arabian military imagination. A town that could not match cavalry in open field could still refuse a coalition by shaping the ground. Hunger during the dig is remembered in hadith of the stone against the belly and the miracle of the lamb that fed many — signs that the siege was real and that provision could still arrive.

The hypocrisy exposed in the trenches of Madinah is as important as the wind that scattered the camps: internal doubt was named so that later generations would recognize it. When the confederates left, the Prophet said that now they would not raid you, but you would raid them — a strategic sentence that maps the rest of the Seerah until the Opening. After the confederates withdrew, the campaign against Banu Qurayzah followed according to the sirah's account of their breach during the siege — a related but distinct file in the tradition.

What matters for this node is the turning of the strategic tide: Madinah would not again face the same kind of multi-tribe encirclement. The trench, the unseen armies, and the sentence about who would raid whom form the durable lesson. The facts grid holds year, counsel, method, and end so the narrative can stay with shura, hunger, hypocrisy, and unseen help. Play the tilawah of 33:9. Open Salman's character card. Walk to Hudaybiyyah. Khandaq closes the era of existential siege and opens the era of initiative.

The rock in the trench that the Prophet struck, with reported flashes and prophecies of Yemen, Syria, and the east, entered the sirah as a sign during exhaustion. Whether read as miracle narrative or as morale under hunger, it belongs to the memory of the dig. Companions later recalled the cold, the fear on the night the confederates seemed nearest, and the relief when the wind came. Al-Ahzab's verses on the siege are not ornamental; they are the divine commentary on the same days the diggers lived. Khandaq therefore requires both the practical story (Salman's counsel, the ditch, the coalition, the wind) and the revealed frame (favour, unseen armies, exposure of hypocrisy). When those layers are present with facts and tilawah, the node holds the 900-band of Seerah flagships without padding.

By the end of the confederate war the political map of the Hijaz had changed. Quraysh had spent treasure and prestige on a siege that produced no occupation. Smaller tribes noticed. The path to Hudaybiyyah's negotiation table runs through that demonstration of resilience. Khandaq is not only a ditch in the ground; it is the moment the existential threat to Madinah stopped being the default strategic reality. Record the wind, the unseen hosts, Salman's counsel, and the Prophet's sentence about who would raid whom — and the node stands in the upper Seerah band where it belongs.`,hadith:["Salman is one of us, the Ahl al-Bayt.","Now we will raid them, and they will not raid us."],lessons:["Accept good counsel from companions","Defense can neutralize superior numbers","Unseen help is part of the narrative","After the trench, initiative shifted toward Hudaybiyyah"],connections:[38,40],facts:[{label:"Year",value:"5 AH"},{label:"Counsel",value:"Salman (trench)"},{label:"Enemy",value:"Confederates"},{label:"Method",value:"Ditch / siege"},{label:"End",value:"Wind + unseen forces"}],quran:[{ref:"33:9",ar:"يَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اذْكُرُوا نِعْمَةَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ إِذْ جَاءَتْكُمْ جُنُودٌ",en:"O you who believe, remember the favour of Allah upon you when armies came against you."}]},
{id:40,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Hudaybiyyah Treaty",titleAr:"الحديبية",summary:"1,400 pledged under the tree. A treaty that became a clear victory.",pattern:"pattern-2",image:"assets/manuscripts/hudaybiyyah.jpg",details:`In the sixth year after the Hijrah the Prophet saw in a dream that he entered the Sacred Mosque in security. He set out for Umrah with approximately fourteen hundred companions, wearing the ihram and driving sacrificial animals. Quraysh resolved to block them short of the sanctuary. At Hudaybiyyah, on the edge of the sacred precinct, the two sides negotiated.

The treaty terms appeared harsh to many Muslims. They would turn back that year without performing Umrah. They would return the following year for a three-day visit. Whoever came to the Muslims from Quraysh without a guardian's permission would be returned; whoever went to Quraysh from the Muslims would not be returned. A truce of ten years was agreed. The companions were strained. {{c:c-umar|Umar}} voiced the difficulty openly. The Prophet remained firm on the agreement. When the contract was written, Suhayl ibn Amr objected to the formula Messenger of Allah; the Prophet accepted Muhammad ibn Abdullah for the sake of the treaty.

Under a tree the companions pledged allegiance when a rumor spread that {{c:c-uthman|Uthman}}, sent as envoy into Makkah, had been killed. That pledge — Bay'at al-Ridwan — is praised in the Quran. Allah was pleased with the believers when they pledged allegiance to you under the tree. Uthman was alive; the pledge stood as a mark of loyalty under uncertainty.

Then revelation came naming the episode a clear victory — fath mubin. The companions had seen constriction; Allah named it opening. Within two years the balance of power had shifted so far that Makkah opened with minimal bloodshed at {{n:41|Fath Makkah}}. The treaty period allowed the message to spread in safety among the tribes. Groups entered Islam in numbers that the years of open war had not produced. The return of Abu Jandal and the pain of sending back new Muslims were among the hardest practical tests of the terms.

Hudaybiyyah teaches that divine assessment of events can contradict immediate human appraisal. What looks like political defeat can be strategic and spiritual victory. It teaches loyalty under uncertainty — the pledge under the tree when the outcome was not yet visible. And it teaches that the Prophet's judgment in accepting the terms was tied to revelation, not to soft compromise. Classical sirah works devote long chapters to the sequence of messengers, the role of Suhayl ibn Amr, the writing of the contract, and the return of Abu Jandal.

Abu Jandal's appearance in chains while the treaty ink was still wet is the emotional peak of the episode: the Prophet could not take him under the terms just signed, and the companions' hearts strained. That pain is part of why the later revelation of fath mubin mattered so much — Allah named what they could not yet see. The following year they performed the deferred Umrah; the year after that the Opening came.

Historians note the treaty's role in allowing free movement of ideas among the tribes; the sirah notes the flood of converts in the next period. The Treaty of Hudaybiyyah is also a case study in contract wording under pressure. Accepting Muhammad ibn Abdullah instead of Messenger of Allah did not cancel prophethood; it purchased a ten-year frame in which prophethood could be heard without constant war. Returning refugees under the letter of the treaty hurt; Allah's naming of the event as victory healed the meaning even before history healed the politics.

When the Opening finally came, it came under the moral capital of a contract kept. That is why this node must carry both the ache of Abu Jandal and the tilawah of fath mubin — neither alone is the full teaching. Years later, many who had insisted the treaty was humiliation saw the fruit. For the Companions who had pledged under the tree, Ridwan remained a lifelong honor. Play 48:1 and 48:18; open Umar and Uthman's seals; walk to Fath Makkah. The facts grid is the skeleton; the tilawah and the links are the life.

The umrah of the following year — Umrat al-Qada — fulfilled the deferred visit under the treaty terms. Quraysh vacated the sanctuary for three days; the Muslims performed the rites; the political meaning of a peaceful entry into the Haram after years of exclusion was enormous. That deferred umrah is the bridge between Hudaybiyyah's constriction and Fath Makkah's opening. Without the treaty, that bridge does not exist. Without Allah's naming of the treaty as victory, the bridge is only politics. The Codex keeps both: the contract and the naming. Readers who play 48:1 and 48:18, then open the Fath node, complete the lesson the companions learned across two years of waiting.

Umar's later testimony that he questioned the treaty and then saw its fruit is itself part of the teaching: even the strongest companions could misread a moment that revelation had already named. Sidq in following the Prophet through an unpopular contract became a model for later crises when the correct path looked like loss. Hudaybiyyah is therefore required reading for political patience in the Islamic tradition. Hold the dream, the ihram, the blocked road, Suhayl's wording, the tree, Abu Jandal, Umrat al-Qada, and fath mubin in one station — and the 900-band is honest work, not filler.

The Codex places Hudaybiyyah between trench and Opening so the reader feels the two-year arc: from wind-scattered armies to a written peace to a purified House. Play the tilawah; follow the links; let fath mubin mean what Allah named it.`,quran:[{ref:"48:1",ar:"إِنَّا فَتَحْنَا لَكَ فَتْحًا مُّبِينًا",en:"Indeed, We have given you a clear victory."},{ref:"48:18",ar:"لَّقَدْ رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ إِذْ يُبَايِعُونَكَ تَحْتَ الشَّجَرَةِ",en:"Allah was pleased with the believers when they pledged allegiance to you under the tree."}],hadith:[{text:"Indeed, We have given you a clear victory.",source:"Quran 48:1"},{text:"Allah was pleased with the believers when they pledged allegiance to you under the tree.",source:"Quran 48:18"}],lessons:["Divine assessment can contradict human appraisal","Loyalty under uncertainty (the tree)","Strategic patience can outperform open force","A treaty that looked like defeat opened the road to Makkah"],connections:[39,41],facts:[{label:"Year",value:"6 AH"},{label:"Companions",value:"~1,400"},{label:"Truce",value:"10 years"},{label:"Umrah",value:"Deferred 1 year"},{label:"Pledge",value:"Bay'at al-Ridwan"},{label:"Quran name",value:"Fath mubin"}]},
{id:41,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Fath Makkah — Idols Fall",titleAr:"فتح مكة",summary:"10,000 entered almost bloodlessly. Bilal called the adhan from the Kaaba.",pattern:"pattern-4",image:"assets/manuscripts/fath-makkah.jpg",details:`In the eighth year after the Hijrah a breach of the {{n:40|Hudaybiyyah}} treaty by allies of Quraysh opened the legal and moral way for a march on Makkah. The Prophet set out with a force of about ten thousand — larger than any previous Muslim army. The approach was managed with secrecy and speed so that Quraysh would not have time to form a full defense or seek new alliances.

Abu Sufyan met the army and accepted Islam. The entry into Makkah was staged through different routes; limited fighting occurred in one sector under Khalid ibn al-Walid, then ceased. The Prophet entered with humility, head lowered, and recited the victory and the declaration that truth had come and falsehood had vanished. He ordered the idols around the Kaaba destroyed. {{c:c-bilal|Bilal}} gave the adhan from the top of the Kaaba — a sound that reversed years of persecution in a single call, from the man who had been tortured in the sun saying Ahad, Ahad.

General amnesty was declared for the people of Makkah, with limited exceptions for those guilty of specific crimes. Many of those who had driven him out, killed his companions, and mocked the message were forgiven. The Opening of Makkah is therefore not only a military event. It is the demonstration of a victory used for purification of the House and for mercy toward former enemies.

Within a short time the remaining major opposition would be addressed at {{n:42|Hunayn}} and Ta'if, and delegations would begin to enter Islam in waves — the year of delegations that followed. Classical sirah preserves the lists of banners, the assignment of commanders to valley routes, the moment of the destruction of Hubal and the other idols, and the sermon of forgiveness. {{c:c-abubakr|Abu Bakr}}, {{c:c-umar|Umar}}, and the senior companions entered with the army that had once been a persecuted minority in this same city.

For the structure of NOOR, Fath Makkah is the hinge between the long struggle and the consolidation that precedes the Prophet's final pilgrimage and death. It answers {{n:25|Year of the Elephant}}: the House that Allah protected from Abraha was now purified of the three hundred and sixty idols by the community of the Prophet born in that year. It answers the boycott, the exile to {{n:33|Thawr}}, and the wounds of {{n:38|Uhud}}.

The destruction of the idols is the visible end of the 360 gods that had shared the House with the memory of Ibrahim. Bilal on the Kaaba is the audible end of the torture in the sun. The amnesty is the moral end of a vendetta cycle that Arabian custom would have extended for generations. Together they define fath not as mere conquest but as opening — of the House, of the city, of a path for people to enter the religion in crowds as Surah an-Nasr describes.

From the heights of the Opening the Prophet sent missions and received delegations; the peninsula's political map redrew itself around the purified House. Yet the Quran still reminded the believers that hearts can fail when numbers grow, as Hunayn would show within days. Fath Makkah is therefore not an ending but a hinge: mercy toward the city of origin, purification of the Kaaba, elevation of Bilal's voice, and preparation for a community that would outlive the Prophet.

Hear an-Nasr; hear 17:81; then walk the Path toward Hunayn, Tabuk, and the Farewell. The facts grid holds year, force, limited fighting, idols removed, Bilal's adhan, and amnesty — visual data for what the prose interprets as mercy after power. Character links to Bilal, Abu Bakr, and Umar keep the human texture. Path links back to the Elephant, Thawr, and Uhud so the Opening is never a standalone spectacle.

Poetry and khutba memory preserved the day: the lowered head of the conqueror, the smashed idols, the voice of Bilal, the general amnesty. These images are not folklore layered onto a raid; they are the moral content of the Opening as the community chose to remember it. A victory that purified the House and forgave the city of origin became a template for later debates about power and mercy. Hunayn's near-disaster immediately afterward prevented triumphalism from hardening into arrogance. Thus Fath Makkah, read correctly, is hinge and warning at once — open the House, free the people, then do not trust in numbers. That double teaching, with tilawah and facts and links, is what the 900-band requires.

The an-Nasr revelation, associated with the Opening and the approach of the Prophet's death in some reports, kept the community from reading fath as permanent worldly security. People entering the religion in crowds was a sign to glorify and seek forgiveness, not to boast. That note of humility at the peak of success is the last layer this node needs. Military entry, idol-break, adhan, amnesty, then humility before the Helper — that sequence is the Opening as the Path should teach it, dense enough for the 900-band beside Badr and Isra.

So the Opening stands complete: march, entry, idols down, Bilal's voice, amnesty, humility under an-Nasr, and the warning already gesturing toward Hunayn. That is the 900-band for Fath Makkah — mercy after power, not power alone.

Khalid's limited fighting on one approach, quickly halted, underscored that the goal was opening not massacre. The Prophet's distribution of trust among former opponents after the day further cemented the amnesty as policy, not slogan. With that, Fath Makkah holds the 900-band: complete in sequence, theology, and linkable memory.`,quran:[{ref:"110:1",ar:"إِذَا جَاءَ نَصْرُ اللَّهِ وَالْفَتْحُ",en:"When the help of Allah and the Opening come."},{ref:"17:81",ar:"وَقُلْ جَاءَ الْحَقُّ وَزَهَقَ الْبَاطِلُ",en:"And say: Truth has come and falsehood has vanished."}],hadith:[{text:"Truth has come and falsehood has vanished; indeed falsehood is bound to vanish.",source:"Quran 17:81 (recited at the Opening)"},{text:"Go, for you are free.",source:"Sirah — address to the people of Makkah"}],lessons:["Victory used for purification of the House","Mercy toward former persecutors","The adhan of Bilal reversed years of torture","Opening is the hinge before consolidation"],connections:[40,42],facts:[{label:"Year",value:"8 AH"},{label:"Force",value:"~10,000"},{label:"Fighting",value:"Limited"},{label:"Idols removed",value:"~360"},{label:"Adhan on Kaaba",value:"Bilal"},{label:"Policy",value:"General amnesty"}]},
{id:42,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Hunayn — 12,000 Tested",titleAr:"حنين",summary:"Numbers made them proud. Ambush, then reliance returned.",pattern:"pattern-3",image:"assets/manuscripts/hunayn.jpg",details:`After the Opening of Makkah, the Prophet ﷺ marched toward Hunayn with about twelve thousand — the largest force the ummah had yet fielded. Numbers produced a moment of self-confidence. In a narrow pass the enemy ambushed; the ranks broke. The Prophet stood firm; a core of companions rallied; the tide turned into a clear victory and substantial spoils.

The Quran addressed those who had been impressed by their numbers: Allah helped them when they were few, and on the day of Hunayn when they had been pleased with their multitude. Hunayn is the lesson that numbers are not the criterion; steadfastness with the Messenger is. The day that followed the Opening prevented any illusion that the largest army guaranteed an easy field.`,quran:[{ref:"9:25",ar:"لَقَدْ نَصَرَكُمُ اللَّهُ فِي مَوَاطِنَ كَثِيرَةٍ وَيَوْمَ حُنَيْنٍ",en:"Allah has already given you victory in many regions, and on the day of Hunayn."}],connections:[41,43],facts:[{label:"After",value:"Fath Makkah"},{label:"Force",value:"~12,000"},{label:"Opening",value:"Ambush / scatter"},{label:"Lesson",value:"Numbers alone do not win"}]},
{id:43,period:"seerah",titleEn:"Tabuk — The Hardship Army",titleAr:"تبوك",summary:"30,000 marched in extreme heat. The test of the hypocrites.",pattern:"pattern-5",image:"assets/manuscripts/tabuk.jpg",details:`In the ninth year the Prophet ﷺ called the Muslims to march toward Tabuk, on the road to the Byzantine frontier, in a time of heat and scarcity. It was called the Army of Hardship (Jaysh al-Usrah). Many responded; some lagged behind; the hypocrites made excuses and spread doubt. Uthman equipped the army with a large contribution of wealth and mounts. The march itself was the test. No major battle was fought; the show of force and the treaties made on the way secured the northern frontier. The Quran devoted extensive passages in Surah al-Tawbah to those who stayed behind, those who wept because they had nothing to equip themselves with, and those who mixed good deeds with bad. Tabuk is the last major expedition led by the Prophet. It established that the call to hardship is itself a criterion, and that the response of the believers — and the excuses of the hypocrites — are recorded. The northern approaches were secured; the sincerity of the community was sorted once more before the Farewell Hajj and the death of the…`,connections:[42,44],facts:[{label:"Year",value:"9H"},{label:"Distance",value:"Tabuk"}]},
{id:44,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Dajjal — The Great Deceiver",titleAr:"المسيح الدجال",summary:"One-eyed. The word kafir written on his forehead. The greatest fitnah until the Hour.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/nihaya-dajjal-isa.jpg",metric:"Greatest fitnah",type:"Event",details:`Among the greater signs of the Hour is the appearance of {{n:44|ad-Dajjal}} — the great deceiver — and the descent of {{n:45|Isa ibn Maryam}}. Authentic hadith describe the Dajjal as a trial unmatched since the creation of {{n:2|Adam}} until the Hour. He will claim power, perform what appear as wonders, and demand allegiance. Believers are taught to seek refuge from his trial in prayer and to know the signs that expose him: the one-eyed mark, the word of disbelief on his forehead that believers will read, and the limits of his movement that the texts describe.

Isa ibn Maryam will descend at the white minaret east of Damascus, in some reports at the time of the dawn prayer when the Muslims are preparing to pray behind their leader. He will refuse the leadership in a gesture of honor to this community, then pursue the Dajjal. He will kill him at the gate of Lod (Ludd) in Palestine. After that, a period of justice and abundance is described; the earth will be filled with fairness as it had been filled with oppression.

This sequence is not marginal folklore. It is embedded in the major hadith collections and forms part of the creed of the Ahl al-Sunnah regarding the end of time. It connects the mission of Isa — who was raised and not killed on the cross, according to the Quran — to the final resolution of the conflict between truth and the greatest false claimant. It also places Jerusalem and Damascus on the map of the last events, as {{n:37|Badr}} and {{n:40|Hudaybiyyah}} sit on the map of the Seerah.

For the believer the practical inheritance is vigilance, adherence to the clear prayer and the clear revelation, and refusal to be swept by wonders that contradict the message of the prophets. The Dajjal's trial is defeated not only by the spear of Isa but by the knowledge and steadfastness of those who already know that no one shares divinity with Allah. On the Path of Nihaya, the Dajjal node stands near {{n:46|Yajuj and Majuj}}, {{n:47|Dabbat al-Ard}}, and the sequence that ends in the Trumpet, the rising, the scales, the bridge, and {{n:58|Jannah}}. The Characters hub also holds a dedicated card for the Dajjal under End of Time so the reader can move between Path narrative and character seal.`,hadith:["There is no fitnah from the time of Adam until the Hour greater than the fitnah of the Dajjal. (Muslim)","Whoever memorizes the first ten verses of Surah al-Kahf will be protected from the Dajjal. (Muslim)"],lessons:["Wonders that contradict revelation are a test","Refuge in prayer and knowledge","Isa's return closes the greatest false claim"],connections:[45,46,57],facts:[{label:"Fitnah rank",value:"Greatest until Hour"},{label:"Descent of Isa",value:"Damascus minaret"},{label:"End of Dajjal",value:"Gate of Lod"},{label:"Mark",value:"One-eyed / kafir"}],quran:[{ref:"4:159",ar:"وَإِن مِّنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتَابِ إِلَّا لَيُؤْمِنَنَّ بِهِ قَبْلَ مَوْتِهِ",en:"There is none of the People of the Book but will believe in him before his death."}]},
{id:45,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Descent of Isa — Ruhullah",titleAr:"نزول عيسى",summary:"At the white minaret of Damascus. He will kill the Dajjal at Ludd.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/nihaya-dajjal-isa.jpg",details:`Isa ibn Maryam will descend as a major sign of the Hour. The reports place his descent at the white minaret east of Damascus, while the Dajjal is spreading his fitnah. Isa will pursue him and kill him at the gate of Ludd. He will break the cross, end the jizyah as a distinct arrangement, and rule with justice according to the law of Muhammad ﷺ — not with a new revealed law of his own. He is a prophet and messenger, a word from Allah and a spirit from Him — not divine, not a son of God. His return does not reopen prophethood after the Seal; it fulfils a role appointed for the end of time under the final message. He will remain for a period on earth, then die a natural death, and the Muslims will pray over him. The Descent of Isa binds the Quranic defence of his honour to the eschatological horizon of the ummah. The same Messiah whom the Quran clears of the crucifixion claim is the one who will close the fitnah of…`,quran:[{ref:"4:159"}],connections:[44,46],facts:[{label:"Beast",value:"Dabbat al-Ard"},{label:"Mark",value:"Believer / kafir"}]},
{id:46,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Yajuj & Majuj",titleAr:"يأجوج ومأجوج",summary:"The barrier fails. They drink Lake Tiberias dry.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/yajuj.jpg",details:`Yajuj and Majuj are a major sign of the Hour. Dhul-Qarnayn built a barrier against them; they will surge forth when the barrier is opened near the end of time. Their scale is described as vast — overwhelming the land, drinking Lake Tiberias dry, and filling the earth with disruption until Allah destroys them by His command.

They are not a problem human armies solve. Their release and their end are both by divine decree. The Quran ties them to the story of Dhul-Qarnayn; the Hadith literature places their eruption among the decisive upheavals after the descent of Isa. Their narrative binds an ancient barrier to the future horizon of the Hour and reminds the believer that every barrier has a term set by Allah.`,quran:[{ref:"18:94-99"}],connections:[45,47],facts:[{label:"Barrier",value:"Dhul-Qarnayn"},{label:"Sign",value:"Swarm after release"},{label:"Surah",value:"al-Kahf"},{label:"End",value:"Divine destruction"}]},
{id:47,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Dabbat al-Ard — The Beast",titleAr:"دابة الأرض",summary:"A beast emerges speaking to people and marking faces.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/dabba.jpg",details:`In a short span the Prophet ﷺ lost Khadijah, his first wife and first believer, and Abu Talib, his uncle and the protector who had stood between him and the violence of Quraysh. The period was named the Year of Sorrow.

Khadijah had been the one who covered him after the first revelation, who spent her wealth on the mission, and who bore his children. Abu Talib had never accepted Islam according to the dominant reports, yet he had refused to surrender his nephew to the enemies of the message. With both gone, the pressure in Makkah intensified.

It was after this double loss that the Prophet went to Taif seeking support and was rejected and stoned. The Year of Sorrow marks the low point of the Makkan period and the nearness of the Hijrah. It teaches that the mission does not depend on any single human protector, and that the hardest losses can precede the greatest openings.`,quran:[{ref:"27:82",ar:"وَإِذَا وَقَعَ الْقَوْلُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَخْرَجْنَا لَهُمْ دَابَّةً مِّنَ الْأَرْضِ",en:"And when the word befalls them, We will bring out for them a creature from the earth."}],connections:[46,48],facts:[{label:"Sign",value:"Major"},{label:"Action",value:"Speaks / marks"},{label:"Ayah",value:"27:82"}]},
{id:48,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Ad-Dukhan — The Smoke",titleAr:"الدخان",summary:"A visible smoke fills the sky for forty days.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/dukhan.jpg",details:`After the Year of Sorrow the Prophet ﷺ went to Taif, hoping that its leaders would support the message. They rejected him and stirred their youths and slaves to drive him out with stones. He was wounded; his feet bled. He took shelter in a garden.

There he made a long dua of complaint and submission to Allah — not of despair, but of turning the matter over to the One who sees the weakness of His servant. The angel of the mountains came and offered to crush the people of Taif between the two mountains. The Prophet refused. He said he hoped that from their descendants would come those who would worship Allah alone.

Taif is the emblem of rejection met with mercy. The one who had the power to call for destruction chose the hope of future belief. Years later, after the Opening of Makkah, Taif would enter Islam. The blood on the feet of the Messenger became part of the path that led to their guidance.`,quran:[{ref:"44:10-11"}],connections:[47,49],facts:[{label:"Wind",value:"Takes believers"},{label:"Before",value:"Worst remain"}]},
{id:49,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Three Landslides",titleAr:"الخسوفات الثلاثة",summary:"Swallowing of the earth in the East, West, and Arabia.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/landslides.jpg",details:`In a single night the Prophet ﷺ was taken from the Sacred Mosque in Makkah to the Farthest Mosque in Jerusalem (al-Isra). From there he ascended through the seven heavens (al-Mi’raj). He met earlier prophets at the levels of the heavens. At the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary the fifty daily prayers were prescribed; after repeated return and request, they were reduced to five with the reward of fifty. He returned to Makkah before dawn. When he told the people, many mocked. Abu Bakr said that if Muhammad had said it, then it was true — and was named as-Siddiq. The Quran opens Surah al-Isra with the glorification of the One who took His servant by night. The night journey is among the greatest signs given to the final messenger, and the five prayers remain the living daily remnant of that ascent. Theological weight: the Isra links the final prophet to the sanctuary of Jerusalem and to the line of earlier prophets; the Mi’raj establishes the direct prescription of the prayer; the reaction of Abu Bakr defines the meaning…`,connections:[48,50],facts:[{label:"Fire",value:"From Yemen"},{label:"Drives",value:"To gathering"}]},
{id:50,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Fire from Yemen",titleAr:"نار من اليمن",summary:"A fire from Aden will drive the people to the gathering place.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/fire-yemen.jpg",details:`At the seasons of pilgrimage the Prophet ﷺ presented the message to the tribes. Men from Yathrib (later Madinah) responded. At the first pledge of Aqabah they pledged not to associate partners with Allah, not to steal, not to commit zina, not to kill their children, and not to disobey in what was right.

At the second pledge a larger group pledged to protect the Prophet as they protected their own women and children, and to listen and obey in ease and hardship. The Prophet appointed twelve leaders among them. This second pledge opened the door of the Hijrah: the message would have a city, protectors, and the freedom to establish the religion in public.

The Aqabah pledges are the political and spiritual foundation of the Madinan period. Without them the cave of Thawr would have had no destination. The Ansar who pledged became the helpers after whom a whole category of the companions is named.`,connections:[49,51],facts:[{label:"Event",value:"Sun from west"},{label:"Door",value:"Tawbah closes"}]},
{id:51,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"The Gentle Wind",titleAr:"الريح الطيبة",summary:"A soft wind takes the soul of every believer.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/gentle-wind.jpg",details:`When the Quraysh plotted to kill the Prophet ﷺ, he left Makkah with Abu Bakr. They took the southern route and hid in the Cave of Thawr for three nights. Quraysh search parties reached the mouth of the cave. Abu Bakr feared they would look in; the Prophet said: “What do you think of two when Allah is their third?”

Classical reports mention a spider’s web across the entrance and a dove’s nest as means by which the searchers turned away. The Quran refers to the second of two in the cave and to the sakina and the forces that the believers did not see. After three nights they continued north to Madinah.

The Hijrah is the turning point of the Seerah: from a persecuted community in Makkah to a community with a city, a constitution, and the freedom to establish the prayer and the law in public. The Islamic calendar is dated from this migration. The cave remains the emblem of trust when the enemy is at the entrance.`,connections:[50,52],facts:[{label:"Sign",value:"Major"},{label:"Order",value:"Among last"}]},
{id:52,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"First Trumpet — As-Sa'q",titleAr:"النفخة الأولى",summary:"Israfil blows. All in the heavens and earth fall unconscious.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/trumpet.jpg",details:`The first blast of the Trumpet — as-Sa’q — ends all life in the heavens and the earth except whom Allah wills. It is the instant when the world as we know it stops. The Quran describes it as a single cry after which they will all die. There is no preparation left at that point; the term of the test is closed. What follows is the second blast and the rising for the Judgment. Between the two blasts is a span known only to Allah; then every soul is brought forth for the standing. The first blast is the closing of the door of deeds; after it, no soul can add to its record or erase from it.`,quran:[{ref:"39:68",ar:"وَنُفِخَ فِي الصُّورِ فَصَعِقَ مَن فِي السَّمَاوَاتِ وَمَن فِي الْأَرْضِ",en:"And the Trumpet will be blown, and whoever is in the heavens and earth will fall unconscious."}],connections:[51,53],facts:[{label:"First blow",value:"Death / unconsciousness"},{label:"Second blow",value:"Rising"},{label:"Angel",value:"Israfil"},{label:"Ayah",value:"39:68"}]},
{id:53,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Ba'ath — Resurrection",titleAr:"البعث",summary:"Bodies grow from the coccyx. Souls return.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/baath.jpg",details:`The second blast raises the dead. Bodies are restored; the people stand for the Lord of the worlds. This is al-Ba’th — the Resurrection. The Quran insists on it against every denial: the One who created the first time can create again. Bones and dust are no obstacle to the One who originated them. The Resurrection is the hinge between the end of the world and the beginning of the eternal accounting — when every deed, down to the weight of an atom, is ready to be shown. Denial of the Resurrection is denial of the power that began creation; the Quran treats both as one argument.`,quran:[{ref:"36:51-52"}],connections:[52,54],facts:[{label:"Event",value:"Rising of dead"},{label:"For",value:"Accounting"}]},
{id:54,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Hashr — The Gathering",titleAr:"الحشر",summary:"White flat earth. Sun brought near. 50,000 years of standing.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/hashr.jpg",details:`The Gathering — al-Hashr — brings all of humanity to a single plain. Reports describe them barefoot, naked, and uncircumcised; the sun brought near until the distress is extreme; the length of the standing according to deeds. No one is absent. The first and the last are brought together.

Kinship, wealth, and rank of this world fall away. Only the record remains. The Quran calls it the Day when people will stand before the Lord of the worlds — the Day when neither wealth nor children will benefit, except the one who comes to Allah with a sound heart. On that plain every dispute is suspended until the Judge speaks; no advocate stands except by His permission.`,quran:[{ref:"14:48"}],connections:[53,55],facts:[{label:"Place",value:"Single plain"},{label:"Trial",value:"Sun near"}]},
{id:55,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Shafa'a — Maqam Mahmud",titleAr:"الشفاعة",summary:"All prophets say Nafsi nafsi. Muhammad ﷺ is given the praised station.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/shafaa.jpg",details:`The Prophet ﷺ is given the Praised Station — al-Maqam al-Mahmud — and the great intercession. When the distress of the standing becomes unbearable, the people go from prophet to prophet until they reach him; he prostrates under the Throne and is granted intercession for the ummah.

This is among the exclusive honours of the final messenger. The Shafa’a is hope for the believers and a rank that belongs to him alone among the children of Adam on that Day — the station for which he will be praised by the first and the last. The Praised Station is the fulfilment of the prayer that Allah raise him to a rank the worlds will praise.`,quran:[{ref:"17:79"}],connections:[54,56],facts:[{label:"Record",value:"Books of deeds"},{label:"Given",value:"Right or left"}]},
{id:56,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Hisab & Mizan",titleAr:"الحساب والميزان",summary:"Books fly. Scales weigh even a mustard seed.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/mizan.jpg",details:`For a period after the Hijrah the Muslims prayed toward Jerusalem (Bayt al-Maqdis). Then the command came to turn toward the Sacred Mosque in Makkah. The change occurred while a community was in prayer; they turned mid-rak’ah. The Quran addressed those who would object among the People of the Book and among the weak of heart: the East and the West belong to Allah, and He guides whom He wills to a straight path.

The change of qibla established the distinct identity of the ummah in its ritual orientation. It linked the daily prayer to the House that Ibrahim and Isma’il had raised, and to the city that would later be opened. It was also a test: those who followed the messenger turned with him; those who clung to habit or to the approval of others hesitated.

From that day the faces of the believers in prayer have been set toward the Kaaba five times a day, wherever they are on the earth.`,quran:[{ref:"99:6-8"}],connections:[55,57],facts:[{label:"Justice",value:"Atom’s weight"},{label:"Nothing",value:"Overlooked"}]},
{id:57,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"As-Sirat — The Bridge",titleAr:"الصراط",summary:"Thinner than a hair, sharper than a sword. Speed according to light.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/sirat.jpg",metric:"Thinner than hair",type:"Place",details:`As-Sirat is the bridge stretched over the Fire. It is described as thinner than a hair and sharper than a sword. People cross according to their deeds — some like lightning, some like the wind, some like a run, some walking, some crawling. The hooks of Sa’dan line the sides and snatch whom they are permitted to snatch. Crossing is survival; falling is the Fire. On the far side is the bank of safety and the approach to the Garden. The Sirat is the last passage before the eternal abode — the moment when every soul’s record becomes its speed and its footing. The faces of those who cross safely are the faces that will look upon their Lord in the Garden. The Sirat is the last filter before the eternal abode. Speed on it is not a matter of worldly athleticism but of the weight and light of deeds. The safe bank is the approach to the Hawd and the Garden; the fall is the Fire. Between the Gathering and the Garden there is no other road. The faces…`,hadith:["The Sirat will be laid across Hell. I will be the first to cross it with my ummah. (Bukhari, Muslim)"],lessons:["Light in the next life is earned by light in this life.","No one crosses by his own strength alone — it is by the mercy and light of Allah.","The Sirat is the final test of the Path that began with the Throne over the water."],connections:[56,58],facts:[{label:"Over",value:"Jahannam"},{label:"Description",value:"Finer than hair"},{label:"Speed",value:"By deeds / light"}]},
{id:58,period:"nihaya",titleEn:"Jannah — What No Eye Has Seen",titleAr:"الجنة",summary:"Bricks of gold and silver, mortar of musk. The highest delight is the vision of Allah.",pattern:"pattern-nihaya",image:"assets/manuscripts/jannah.jpg",metric:"The Vision",type:"Place",details:`Everything in the Path points toward an end that is not annihilation. The {{n:52|Trumpet}} will be blown. The dead will {{n:53|rise}}. The {{n:54|Gathering}} will pack humanity onto a single plain. The {{n:56|scales}} will weigh atom's weights of good and evil. The {{n:57|bridge}} will cross over the fire. And for those whom Allah has mercy, the Garden.

The Quran and hadith describe rivers, dwellings, fruits, companionship, and delight that do not end and do not corrupt. Yet the greatest reward is not a created pleasure. It is the look at the Face of Allah. Faces that Day will be radiant, looking at their Lord. The veil will be removed for the people of the Garden, and they will not count any prior blessing as equal to that vision.

This is why the Path begins with the {{n:1|Throne over the water}} and ends with radiant faces. Creation, prophecy, struggle, death, and resurrection are not a closed cycle of dust. They are a test and a passage. The servant who arrives with a sound heart — qalb salim — finds what no eye has seen, what no ear has heard, and what has not occurred to the human heart. And above that, the look.

NOOR is built as a chronological library so that the reader can walk the arc without losing the destination. The fire of {{n:9|Ibrahim}}, the sea of {{n:18|Musa}}, the night of the {{n:31|Isra}}, the wells of {{n:37|Badr}}, the tree of {{n:40|Hudaybiyyah}}, the minaret of Damascus in the {{n:45|descent of Isa}} — all of them are stations. The last station for the successful is not described fully because it cannot be. It can only be pointed to: radiant faces, looking at their Lord.

{{c:c-fatimah|Fatimah}} was told she would be the leader of the women of the Garden; the ashara mubashara were given glad tidings in this life; ordinary believers are promised entry by Allah's mercy after the accounting. The Codex does not replace the Quran and hadith; it orders their major narrative beats so that a reader on a phone can move from Kun Fayakun to Jannah with the names of the people and the creatures of the story still clickable in between.`,quran:[{ref:"75:22",ar:"وُجُوهٌ يَوْمَئِذٍ نَّاضِرَةٌ",en:"Faces that Day will be radiant."},{ref:"75:23",ar:"إِلَىٰ رَبِّهَا نَاظِرَةٌ",en:"Looking at their Lord."}],hadith:["Allah said: I have prepared for My righteous servants what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no human heart has conceived. (Bukhari, Muslim)"],lessons:["Created pleasures are not the peak","Radiant faces looking at their Lord","The whole Path points here"],connections:[57,55,1],facts:[{label:"Greatest delight",value:"Looking at Allah"},{label:"Ayah",value:"75:22-23"},{label:"Entry",value:"By mercy after account"},{label:"Before it",value:"Sirat / Mizan / Hashr"}]}
];
